const FAM_WEEKS={"46": {"t": "“There Is No Other God That Can Deliver”", "b": "Daniel 1–7", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/46?lang=eng"}, "47": {"t": "“I Will Love Them Freely”", "b": "Hosea 1–6; 10–14; Joel", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/47?lang=eng"}, "48": {"t": "“Seek the Lord, and Ye Shall Live”", "b": "Amos; Obadiah; Jonah", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/48?lang=eng"}, "49": {"t": "“He Delighteth in Mercy”", "b": "Micah; Nahum; Habakkuk; Zephaniah", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/49?lang=eng"}, "50": {"t": "“Holiness unto the Lord”", "b": "Haggai 1–2; Zechariah 1–4; 7–14", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/50?lang=eng"}, "51": {"t": "“I Have Loved You, Saith the Lord”", "b": "Malachi", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/51?lang=eng"}, "52": {"t": "“We Have Waited for Him, and He Will Save Us”", "b": "Christmas", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/52?lang=eng"}};
const FAM=[{"d": "2026-11-15", "w": 46, "t": "Daniel keeps praying", "a": [{"r": "Daniel 6:10, 22", "v": [[6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."]], "q": "Daniel prayed three times a day, even when the king made a rule against it. When are times you can pray?", "act": "Take turns kneeling the way Daniel did, then have a child say the family prayer."}, {"r": "Daniel 6:10, 16, 19–22", "v": [[6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 16, "Then the king commanded, and they brought Daniel, and cast him into the den of lions. Now the king spake and said unto Daniel, Thy God whom thou servest continually, he will deliver thee."], [6, 19, "Then the king arose very early in the morning, and went in haste unto the den of lions."], [6, 20, "And when he came to the den, he cried with a lamentable voice unto Daniel: and the king spake and said to Daniel, O Daniel, servant of the living God, is thy God, whom thou servest continually, able to deliver thee from the lions?"], [6, 21, "Then said Daniel unto the king, O king, live for ever."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."]], "q": "Daniel knew about the new law and still prayed with his windows open. Why do you think he didn't hide?"}, {"r": "Daniel 6:4–10, 16–23", "v": [[6, 4, "Then the presidents and princes sought to find occasion against Daniel concerning the kingdom; but they could find none occasion nor fault; forasmuch as he was faithful, neither was there any error or fault found in him."], [6, 5, "Then said these men, We shall not find any occasion against this Daniel, except we find it against him concerning the law of his God."], [6, 6, "Then these presidents and princes assembled together to the king, and said thus unto him, King Darius, live for ever."], [6, 7, "All the presidents of the kingdom, the governors, and the princes, the counsellors, and the captains, have consulted together to establish a royal statute, and to make a firm decree, that whosoever shall ask a petition of any God or man for thirty days, save of thee, O king, he shall be cast into the den of lions."], [6, 8, "Now, O king, establish the decree, and sign the writing, that it be not changed, according to the law of the Medes and Persians, which altereth not."], [6, 9, "Wherefore king Darius signed the writing and the decree."], [6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 16, "Then the king commanded, and they brought Daniel, and cast him into the den of lions. Now the king spake and said unto Daniel, Thy God whom thou servest continually, he will deliver thee."], [6, 17, "And a stone was brought, and laid upon the mouth of the den; and the king sealed it with his own signet, and with the signet of his lords; that the purpose might not be changed concerning Daniel."], [6, 18, "Then the king went to his palace, and passed the night fasting: neither were instruments of musick brought before him: and his sleep went from him."], [6, 19, "Then the king arose very early in the morning, and went in haste unto the den of lions."], [6, 20, "And when he came to the den, he cried with a lamentable voice unto Daniel: and the king spake and said to Daniel, O Daniel, servant of the living God, is thy God, whom thou servest continually, able to deliver thee from the lions?"], [6, 21, "Then said Daniel unto the king, O king, live for ever."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."], [6, 23, "Then was the king exceeding glad for him, and commanded that they should take Daniel up out of the den. So Daniel was taken up out of the den, and no manner of hurt was found upon him, because he believed in his God."]], "q": "His enemies said the only way to catch Daniel was through “the law of his God” (verse 5). What does that tell you about how he lived?"}, {"r": "Daniel 6:1–23", "v": [[6, 1, "It pleased Darius to set over the kingdom an hundred and twenty princes, which should be over the whole kingdom;"], [6, 2, "And over these three presidents; of whom Daniel was first: that the princes might give accounts unto them, and the king should have no damage."], [6, 3, "Then this Daniel was preferred above the presidents and princes, because an excellent spirit was in him; and the king thought to set him over the whole realm."], [6, 4, "Then the presidents and princes sought to find occasion against Daniel concerning the kingdom; but they could find none occasion nor fault; forasmuch as he was faithful, neither was there any error or fault found in him."], [6, 5, "Then said these men, We shall not find any occasion against this Daniel, except we find it against him concerning the law of his God."], [6, 6, "Then these presidents and princes assembled together to the king, and said thus unto him, King Darius, live for ever."], [6, 7, "All the presidents of the kingdom, the governors, and the princes, the counsellors, and the captains, have consulted together to establish a royal statute, and to make a firm decree, that whosoever shall ask a petition of any God or man for thirty days, save of thee, O king, he shall be cast into the den of lions."], [6, 8, "Now, O king, establish the decree, and sign the writing, that it be not changed, according to the law of the Medes and Persians, which altereth not."], [6, 9, "Wherefore king Darius signed the writing and the decree."], [6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 11, "Then these men assembled, and found Daniel praying and making supplication before his God."], [6, 12, "Then they came near, and spake before the king concerning the king’s decree; Hast thou not signed a decree, that every man that shall ask a petition of any God or man within thirty days, save of thee, O king, shall be cast into the den of lions? The king answered and said, The thing is true, according to the law of the Medes and Persians, which altereth not."], [6, 13, "Then answered they and said before the king, That Daniel, which is of the children of the captivity of Judah, regardeth not thee, O king, nor the decree that thou hast signed, but maketh his petition three times a day."], [6, 14, "Then the king, when he heard these words, was sore displeased with himself, and set his heart on Daniel to deliver him: and he laboured till the going down of the sun to deliver him."], [6, 15, "Then these men assembled unto the king, and said unto the king, Know, O king, that the law of the Medes and Persians is, That no decree nor statute which the king establisheth may be changed."], [6, 16, "Then the king commanded, and they brought Daniel, and cast him into the den of lions. Now the king spake and said unto Daniel, Thy God whom thou servest continually, he will deliver thee."], [6, 17, "And a stone was brought, and laid upon the mouth of the den; and the king sealed it with his own signet, and with the signet of his lords; that the purpose might not be changed concerning Daniel."], [6, 18, "Then the king went to his palace, and passed the night fasting: neither were instruments of musick brought before him: and his sleep went from him."], [6, 19, "Then the king arose very early in the morning, and went in haste unto the den of lions."], [6, 20, "And when he came to the den, he cried with a lamentable voice unto Daniel: and the king spake and said to Daniel, O Daniel, servant of the living God, is thy God, whom thou servest continually, able to deliver thee from the lions?"], [6, 21, "Then said Daniel unto the king, O king, live for ever."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."], [6, 23, "Then was the king exceeding glad for him, and commanded that they should take Daniel up out of the den. So Daniel was taken up out of the den, and no manner of hurt was found upon him, because he believed in his God."]], "q": "Daniel prayed “as he did aforetime” (verse 10). The habit was already there before the crisis came. Which habits keep your family steady when pressure comes?"}], "ck": "Daniel is an old man now, and a new king named Darius rules. Some jealous leaders trick the king into making a law against praying to anyone but him.", "cf": "Babylon has fallen to the Medes and Persians (539 BC), and Daniel, now in his 80s, serves King Darius. Jealous officials can't find any fault in his work, so they target his faith with a law that, once signed, can't be changed.", "wd": ["den", "גֹּב", "gob", "Aramaic", "a pit. Lions were kept in a pit in the ground, and a stone was rolled over the opening (verse 17)."]}, {"d": "2026-11-16", "w": 47, "t": "God loves us freely", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 14:4", "v": [[14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."]], "q": "God says, “I will love them freely.” What does it feel like when someone loves you even after you make a mistake?", "act": "Go around the room and give each person a hug and say, “I love you, no matter what.”"}, {"r": "Hosea 14:1–2, 4", "v": [[14, 1, "O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God; for thou hast fallen by thine iniquity."], [14, 2, "Take with you words, and turn to the LORD: say unto him, Take away all iniquity, and receive us graciously: so will we render the calves of our lips."], [14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."]], "q": "God asks Israel to “return” to Him. What does it mean to come back to God after we do something wrong?"}, {"r": "Hosea 14:1–4", "v": [[14, 1, "O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God; for thou hast fallen by thine iniquity."], [14, 2, "Take with you words, and turn to the LORD: say unto him, Take away all iniquity, and receive us graciously: so will we render the calves of our lips."], [14, 3, "Asshur shall not save us; we will not ride upon horses: neither will we say any more to the work of our hands, Ye are our gods: for in thee the fatherless findeth mercy."], [14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."]], "q": "Verse 2 says to “take with you words” when you turn back to the Lord. What words would you say to Him?"}, {"r": "Hosea 14:1–9", "v": [[14, 1, "O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God; for thou hast fallen by thine iniquity."], [14, 2, "Take with you words, and turn to the LORD: say unto him, Take away all iniquity, and receive us graciously: so will we render the calves of our lips."], [14, 3, "Asshur shall not save us; we will not ride upon horses: neither will we say any more to the work of our hands, Ye are our gods: for in thee the fatherless findeth mercy."], [14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."], [14, 5, "I will be as the dew unto Israel: he shall grow as the lily, and cast forth his roots as Lebanon."], [14, 6, "His branches shall spread, and his beauty shall be as the olive tree, and his smell as Lebanon."], [14, 7, "They that dwell under his shadow shall return; they shall revive as the corn, and grow as the vine: the scent thereof shall be as the wine of Lebanon."], [14, 8, "Ephraim shall say, What have I to do any more with idols? I have heard him, and observed him: I am like a green fir tree. From me is thy fruit found."], [14, 9, "Who is wise, and he shall understand these things? prudent, and he shall know them? for the ways of the LORD are right, and the just shall walk in them: but the transgressors shall fall therein."]], "q": "Hosea 14 promises healing, not just forgiveness (verses 4–7). What pictures does God use, and what do they say about how He helps us change?"}], "ck": "Hosea lived in Israel long ago. His people kept turning away from God, but God kept inviting them to come back.", "cf": "Hosea preached in the northern kingdom of Israel around 750–725 BC, shortly before Assyria conquered it in 722 BC. God had him marry Gomer, who left him; Hosea took her back. His marriage became a picture of how God keeps reaching for His people.", "wd": ["return", "שׁוּב", "shuv", "Hebrew", "to turn around and come back. It's the Old Testament's main word for repenting."]}, {"d": "2026-11-17", "w": 47, "t": "Keeping our promises with God", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 2:20", "v": [[2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "God promises to be faithful to us. Being faithful means keeping your promises. What is one promise you can keep this week?", "act": "Pinky-promise one kind thing you will do for someone in the family before bedtime."}, {"r": "Hosea 2:19–20", "v": [[2, 19, "And I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies."], [2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "God compares His promises with us to a marriage. Which words in these verses tell how He treats us?"}, {"r": "Hosea 2:19–20", "v": [[2, 19, "And I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies."], [2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "“Betroth” means to promise to marry. Why might God describe His covenant with us like a marriage instead of a contract?"}, {"r": "Hosea 2:14–20", "v": [[2, 14, "Therefore, behold, I will allure her, and bring her into the wilderness, and speak comfortably unto her."], [2, 15, "And I will give her her vineyards from thence, and the valley of Achor for a door of hope: and she shall sing there, as in the days of her youth, and as in the day when she came up out of the land of Egypt."], [2, 16, "And it shall be at that day, saith the LORD, that thou shalt call me Ishi; and shalt call me no more Baali."], [2, 17, "For I will take away the names of Baalim out of her mouth, and they shall no more be remembered by their name."], [2, 18, "And in that day will I make a covenant for them with the beasts of the field, and with the fowls of heaven, and with the creeping things of the ground: and I will break the bow and the sword and the battle out of the earth, and will make them to lie down safely."], [2, 19, "And I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies."], [2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "God promises to “speak comfortably” and turn a valley of trouble into “a door of hope” (verses 14–15). Where have you seen Him do that?"}], "ck": "God told Hosea to love his wife even when she left. That showed how God keeps loving His people.", "cf": "Hosea's own marriage, broken and then restored, is the backdrop here. God speaks of Israel as a bride He will win back, not with force but with faithfulness.", "wd": ["lovingkindness", "חֶסֶד", "chesed", "Hebrew", "loyal, covenant love: love that keeps its promises even when the other person doesn't."]}, {"d": "2026-11-18", "w": 47, "t": "Goodness that lasts", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 6:6", "v": [[6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "God says He wants “mercy,” which means being kind and forgiving. How can you be kind to someone today?", "act": "Pick one person to secretly do something kind for today."}, {"r": "Hosea 6:4, 6", "v": [[6, 4, "O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away."], [6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "Verse 4 says the people's goodness disappeared like morning dew. What would it look like for our goodness to last all day?"}, {"r": "Hosea 6:1–6", "v": [[6, 1, "Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up."], [6, 2, "After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight."], [6, 3, "Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the LORD: his going forth is prepared as the morning; and he shall come unto us as the rain, as the latter and former rain unto the earth."], [6, 4, "O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away."], [6, 5, "Therefore have I hewed them by the prophets; I have slain them by the words of my mouth: and thy judgments are as the light that goeth forth."], [6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "In verse 6 God wants mercy and knowing Him more than offerings. What's the difference between doing religious things and actually knowing God?"}, {"r": "Hosea 6:1–6", "v": [[6, 1, "Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up."], [6, 2, "After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight."], [6, 3, "Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the LORD: his going forth is prepared as the morning; and he shall come unto us as the rain, as the latter and former rain unto the earth."], [6, 4, "O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away."], [6, 5, "Therefore have I hewed them by the prophets; I have slain them by the words of my mouth: and thy judgments are as the light that goeth forth."], [6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "Their goodness was “as a morning cloud” (verse 4). What helps devotion last past Sunday in our home?"}], "ck": "In Hosea's day, people still made offerings at the temple, but they weren't really being kind or loving God.", "cf": "Israel kept up sacrifices and festivals while being dishonest and unkind. Dew and morning clouds in that land burn off fast in the sun, and Hosea says their goodness disappears just as quickly.", "wd": ["knowledge", "דַּעַת", "da'at", "Hebrew", "knowing someone personally, from experience, not just knowing facts about them."]}, {"d": "2026-11-19", "w": 47, "t": "Time to seek the Lord", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 10:12", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."]], "q": "Hosea talks about planting seeds. What good things can we plant in our family, like kindness or prayer?", "act": "Draw a seed growing into a plant, and write on it one good thing you want to grow."}, {"r": "Hosea 10:12", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."]], "q": "“Break up your fallow ground” means getting hard, unused dirt ready to plant. What helps soften our hearts so good things can grow?"}, {"r": "Hosea 10:12–13", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."], [10, 13, "Ye have plowed wickedness, ye have reaped iniquity; ye have eaten the fruit of lies: because thou didst trust in thy way, in the multitude of thy mighty men."]], "q": "Hosea says “it is time to seek the LORD.” Why might now be the time, instead of later?"}, {"r": "Hosea 10:12–13", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."], [10, 13, "Ye have plowed wickedness, ye have reaped iniquity; ye have eaten the fruit of lies: because thou didst trust in thy way, in the multitude of thy mighty men."]], "q": "Verse 13 says Israel trusted “in thy way” and reaped what they planted. What are we planting as a family right now, and what will it grow?"}], "ck": "Farmers in Israel had to plow hard, dry ground before the rain came, or nothing would grow.", "cf": "After the long dry summer, farmers broke up hardened ground so the autumn rains could soak in. Hosea uses farming to describe a heart that's ready for God.", "wd": ["rain", "יֹרֶה", "yoreh", "Hebrew", "the early rain, but the same word can also mean “to teach.” So God will “rain” and “teach” righteousness."]}, {"d": "2026-11-20", "w": 47, "t": "Jesus is our Savior", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 13:4", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."]], "q": "God says, “There is no saviour beside me.” Jesus is our Savior. What has Jesus done for you?", "act": "Look at a picture of Jesus and have each person name one thing they love about Him."}, {"r": "Hosea 13:4, 14", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."], [13, 14, "I will ransom them from the power of the grave; I will redeem them from death: O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction: repentance shall be hid from mine eyes."]], "q": "In verse 14 God promises to “redeem them from death.” How did Jesus make that possible?"}, {"r": "Hosea 13:4, 9, 14", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."], [13, 9, "O Israel, thou hast destroyed thyself; but in me is thine help."], [13, 14, "I will ransom them from the power of the grave; I will redeem them from death: O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction: repentance shall be hid from mine eyes."]], "q": "Verse 9 says, “in me is thine help.” Where do people look for help instead of God, and why does Hosea say only He can save?"}, {"r": "Hosea 13:4–6, 9, 14", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."], [13, 5, "I did know thee in the wilderness, in the land of great drought."], [13, 6, "According to their pasture, so were they filled; they were filled, and their heart was exalted; therefore have they forgotten me."], [13, 9, "O Israel, thou hast destroyed thyself; but in me is thine help."], [13, 14, "I will ransom them from the power of the grave; I will redeem them from death: O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction: repentance shall be hid from mine eyes."]], "q": "Israel forgot God once they were full and comfortable (verse 6). Why is it easier to forget God when life is going well?"}], "ck": "Long ago God saved Israel from slavery in Egypt. Hosea reminds them that God is the one who saves.", "cf": "Hosea points back to the Exodus: the God who led Israel out of Egypt is their only Savior. Paul later quotes Hosea 13:14 when he writes about the Resurrection: “O death, where is thy sting?” (1 Corinthians 15:55).", "wd": ["redeem", "גָּאַל", "ga'al", "Hebrew", "to buy back. A redeemer was a close relative who paid to free a family member or win back lost land."]}, {"d": "2026-11-21", "w": 47, "t": "Turning with all our heart", "a": [{"r": "Joel 2:13", "v": [[2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "God is “slow to anger, and of great kindness.” Who do you know who is slow to get angry? How can we be like that?", "act": "Practice taking three slow breaths together, something to do when you start to feel angry."}, {"r": "Joel 2:12–13", "v": [[2, 12, "Therefore also now, saith the LORD, turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:"], [2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "People used to tear their clothes to show they were sorry. Why does God ask us to “rend your heart, and not your garments”?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:12–13", "v": [[2, 12, "Therefore also now, saith the LORD, turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:"], [2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "What's the difference between looking sorry and actually turning back to God “with all your heart”?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:12–13", "v": [[2, 12, "Therefore also now, saith the LORD, turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:"], [2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "Joel describes God as “gracious and merciful, slow to anger.” How does knowing that change the way we repent?"}], "ck": "A huge swarm of locusts, like giant grasshoppers, ate all the crops. The prophet Joel told the people to turn back to God.", "cf": "Joel 1 describes a locust plague that stripped the land bare. Joel uses it as a wake-up call. In that culture, people tore their clothes to show grief, but God asks for more than an outward show.", "wd": ["rend", "קָרַע", "qara", "Hebrew", "to tear. People tore their clothes in sorrow; God asks them to tear open their hearts instead."]}, {"d": "2026-11-22", "w": 47, "t": "The Spirit for everyone", "a": [{"r": "Joel 2:28", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"]], "q": "God promised to pour out His Spirit, the Holy Ghost, on everyone, even sons and daughters. How does the Holy Ghost help you?", "act": "Slowly pour water into a cup while you talk about how the Holy Ghost can fill us with peace."}, {"r": "Joel 2:28–29", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"], [2, 29, "And also upon the servants and upon the handmaids in those days will I pour out my spirit."]], "q": "Joel says young and old, sons and daughters, will all receive the Spirit. What does that tell you about who God speaks to?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:28–32", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"], [2, 29, "And also upon the servants and upon the handmaids in those days will I pour out my spirit."], [2, 30, "And I will shew wonders in the heavens and in the earth, blood, and fire, and pillars of smoke."], [2, 31, "The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and the terrible day of the LORD come."], [2, 32, "And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered: for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be deliverance, as the LORD hath said, and in the remnant whom the LORD shall call."]], "q": "Peter quoted this prophecy on the day of Pentecost (Acts 2:16–18). When have you seen the Spirit come to ordinary people?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:28–32", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"], [2, 29, "And also upon the servants and upon the handmaids in those days will I pour out my spirit."], [2, 30, "And I will shew wonders in the heavens and in the earth, blood, and fire, and pillars of smoke."], [2, 31, "The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and the terrible day of the LORD come."], [2, 32, "And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered: for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be deliverance, as the LORD hath said, and in the remnant whom the LORD shall call."]], "q": "“Whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered” (verse 32). What does calling on His name look like in daily life?"}], "ck": "After the locusts, God promised good things would come back, and that His Spirit would come to everyone.", "cf": "After promising to restore what the locusts ate (2:25), Joel looks further ahead. Peter quoted this prophecy on the day of Pentecost (Acts 2:16–21), and the angel Moroni quoted it to Joseph Smith, saying it was about to be fulfilled (Joseph Smith—History 1:41).", "wd": ["pour out", "שָׁפַךְ", "shaphak", "Hebrew", "to pour out generously, the way you'd empty a full pitcher, not a few drops."]}, {"d": "2026-11-23", "w": 48, "t": "Prophets share God's messages", "a": [{"r": "Amos 3:7", "v": [[3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."]], "q": "God tells His plans to prophets. Who is the prophet today, and what has he taught us?", "act": "Play a quick game of telephone, then talk about why we listen to the prophet instead of what people pass around."}, {"r": "Amos 3:7–8", "v": [[3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."], [3, 8, "The lion hath roared, who will not fear? the Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?"]], "q": "Amos asks, “The Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?” Why would a prophet feel he has to share God's message?"}, {"r": "Amos 3:3–8", "v": [[3, 3, "Can two walk together, except they be agreed?"], [3, 4, "Will a lion roar in the forest, when he hath no prey? will a young lion cry out of his den, if he have taken nothing?"], [3, 5, "Can a bird fall in a snare upon the earth, where no gin is for him? shall one take up a snare from the earth, and have taken nothing at all?"], [3, 6, "Shall a trumpet be blown in the city, and the people not be afraid? shall there be evil in a city, and the LORD hath not done it?"], [3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."], [3, 8, "The lion hath roared, who will not fear? the Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?"]], "q": "Amos lists cause-and-effect questions, like a lion roaring because it has caught something. What point is he making about why prophets speak?"}, {"r": "Amos 3:3–8", "v": [[3, 3, "Can two walk together, except they be agreed?"], [3, 4, "Will a lion roar in the forest, when he hath no prey? will a young lion cry out of his den, if he have taken nothing?"], [3, 5, "Can a bird fall in a snare upon the earth, where no gin is for him? shall one take up a snare from the earth, and have taken nothing at all?"], [3, 6, "Shall a trumpet be blown in the city, and the people not be afraid? shall there be evil in a city, and the LORD hath not done it?"], [3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."], [3, 8, "The lion hath roared, who will not fear? the Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?"]], "q": "“Can two walk together, except they be agreed?” (verse 3). What does walking with God ask of us?"}], "ck": "Amos was a shepherd who also took care of fig trees. God called him to be a prophet.", "cf": "Amos came from Tekoa in Judah and worked as a herdsman and fig farmer (Amos 1:1; 7:14). Around 760 BC God sent him north to Israel during a time of wealth and comfort, when few wanted to hear a warning.", "wd": ["secret", "סוֹד", "sod", "Hebrew", "a council, or the private plans shared in it. Prophets are invited into God's council."]}, {"d": "2026-11-24", "w": 48, "t": "Seek the Lord and live", "a": [{"r": "Amos 5:4", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"]], "q": "God says, “Seek ye me, and ye shall live.” Seek means look for. Where can we look for God?", "act": "Hide a picture of Jesus and let the children find it. Then talk about ways we can look for Him."}, {"r": "Amos 5:4, 14", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"], [5, 14, "Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken."]], "q": "“Seek good, and not evil.” What is one good thing our family could seek this week?"}, {"r": "Amos 5:4–8, 14–15", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"], [5, 5, "But seek not Beth-el, nor enter into Gilgal, and pass not to Beer-sheba: for Gilgal shall surely go into captivity, and Beth-el shall come to nought."], [5, 6, "Seek the LORD, and ye shall live; lest he break out like fire in the house of Joseph, and devour it, and there be none to quench it in Beth-el."], [5, 7, "Ye who turn judgment to wormwood, and leave off righteousness in the earth,"], [5, 8, "Seek him that maketh the seven stars and Orion, and turneth the shadow of death into the morning, and maketh the day dark with night: that calleth for the waters of the sea, and poureth them out upon the face of the earth: The LORD is his name:"], [5, 14, "Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken."], [5, 15, "Hate the evil, and love the good, and establish judgment in the gate: it may be that the LORD God of hosts will be gracious unto the remnant of Joseph."]], "q": "In verse 8, Amos points to the God who made the stars. How does remembering how great God is help us seek Him?"}, {"r": "Amos 5:4–15", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"], [5, 5, "But seek not Beth-el, nor enter into Gilgal, and pass not to Beer-sheba: for Gilgal shall surely go into captivity, and Beth-el shall come to nought."], [5, 6, "Seek the LORD, and ye shall live; lest he break out like fire in the house of Joseph, and devour it, and there be none to quench it in Beth-el."], [5, 7, "Ye who turn judgment to wormwood, and leave off righteousness in the earth,"], [5, 8, "Seek him that maketh the seven stars and Orion, and turneth the shadow of death into the morning, and maketh the day dark with night: that calleth for the waters of the sea, and poureth them out upon the face of the earth: The LORD is his name:"], [5, 9, "That strengtheneth the spoiled against the strong, so that the spoiled shall come against the fortress."], [5, 10, "They hate him that rebuketh in the gate, and they abhor him that speaketh uprightly."], [5, 11, "Forasmuch therefore as your treading is upon the poor, and ye take from him burdens of wheat: ye have built houses of hewn stone, but ye shall not dwell in them; ye have planted pleasant vineyards, but ye shall not drink wine of them."], [5, 12, "For I know your manifold transgressions and your mighty sins: they afflict the just, they take a bribe, and they turn aside the poor in the gate from their right."], [5, 13, "Therefore the prudent shall keep silence in that time; for it is an evil time."], [5, 14, "Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken."], [5, 15, "Hate the evil, and love the good, and establish judgment in the gate: it may be that the LORD God of hosts will be gracious unto the remnant of Joseph."]], "q": "Amos ties seeking God to treating the poor fairly (verses 11–12, 15). Why are worship and how we treat others connected?"}], "ck": "People in Israel were going to special places to worship idols. Amos told them to look for God instead.", "cf": "Bethel, Gilgal and Beersheba were famous shrines, and at Bethel Israel worshiped a golden calf. Amos says going to the right place means nothing if you aren't seeking the Lord and treating people justly.", "wd": ["seek", "דָּרַשׁ", "darash", "Hebrew", "to search carefully, to ask and keep asking, the way you'd look for something you really need."]}, {"d": "2026-11-25", "w": 48, "t": "Hungry for God's word", "a": [{"r": "Amos 8:11", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"]], "q": "Amos talks about being hungry, not for food but for God's words. What words of God do you love to hear?", "act": "Share a favorite snack, and let each person share a favorite scripture or song while you eat."}, {"r": "Amos 8:11–12", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"], [8, 12, "And they shall wander from sea to sea, and from the north even to the east, they shall run to and fro to seek the word of the LORD, and shall not find it."]], "q": "What would it be like if no one could find the scriptures or a prophet? How are we blessed to have them?"}, {"r": "Amos 8:11–12", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"], [8, 12, "And they shall wander from sea to sea, and from the north even to the east, they shall run to and fro to seek the word of the LORD, and shall not find it."]], "q": "People “run to and fro” looking for God's word and can't find it. How did the Restoration end that kind of famine?"}, {"r": "Amos 8:11–12", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"], [8, 12, "And they shall wander from sea to sea, and from the north even to the east, they shall run to and fro to seek the word of the LORD, and shall not find it."]], "q": "Where do you see spiritual hunger in the people around you, and how can our family share what fills it?"}], "ck": "Israel was rich and had plenty of food, but they weren't listening to God's prophets.", "cf": "Amos spoke during good economic times. He warns that a day would come when people would want God's word and not be able to find it, a famine worse than running out of bread.", "wd": ["famine", "רָעָב", "ra'av", "Hebrew", "hunger or starvation. The same word is used for real famines of food, which makes Amos's picture hit hard."]}, {"d": "2026-11-26", "w": 48, "t": "Helping our ancestors", "a": [{"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "Prophets have taught that when we do temple work for people who have died, we help them like Jesus helps us. Who in our family history would you like to learn about?", "act": "Look at a photo of a grandparent or ancestor and share one story about them."}, {"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "“Saviours shall come up on mount Zion.” How is doing temple work for someone who died like what Jesus does for us?"}, {"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "How can family history or doing baptisms for the dead let you help save someone?"}, {"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "On Thanksgiving: which ancestors are you grateful for today, and what do you know about their lives?"}], "ck": "Obadiah is the shortest book in the Old Testament. It's only one chapter long!", "cf": "Obadiah's 21 verses are about Edom, the descendants of Esau, who cheered when Jerusalem was attacked. The book ends with hope: saviors on Mount Zion and the kingdom belonging to the Lord.", "wd": ["saviours", "מוֹשִׁעִים", "moshi'im", "Hebrew", "“those who save,” from yasha, “to save.” The names Joshua and Jesus come from the same root."]}, {"d": "2026-11-27", "w": 48, "t": "Jonah runs away", "a": [{"r": "Jonah 1:3, 17", "v": [[1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "God asked Jonah to go to Nineveh, but Jonah went the other way. What happened next?", "act": "Act out the story: one person is the ship, one the storm, and one the big fish."}, {"r": "Jonah 1:1–4, 15–17", "v": [[1, 1, "Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai, saying,"], [1, 2, "Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me."], [1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 4, "But the LORD sent out a great wind into the sea, and there was a mighty tempest in the sea, so that the ship was like to be broken."], [1, 15, "So they took up Jonah, and cast him forth into the sea: and the sea ceased from her raging."], [1, 16, "Then the men feared the LORD exceedingly, and offered a sacrifice unto the LORD, and made vows."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "Why do you think Jonah didn't want to go? Have you ever not wanted to do something God asked?"}, {"r": "Jonah 1:1–17", "v": [[1, 1, "Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai, saying,"], [1, 2, "Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me."], [1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 4, "But the LORD sent out a great wind into the sea, and there was a mighty tempest in the sea, so that the ship was like to be broken."], [1, 5, "Then the mariners were afraid, and cried every man unto his god, and cast forth the wares that were in the ship into the sea, to lighten it of them. But Jonah was gone down into the sides of the ship; and he lay, and was fast asleep."], [1, 6, "So the shipmaster came to him, and said unto him, What meanest thou, O sleeper? arise, call upon thy God, if so be that God will think upon us, that we perish not."], [1, 7, "And they said every one to his fellow, Come, and let us cast lots, that we may know for whose cause this evil is upon us. So they cast lots, and the lot fell upon Jonah."], [1, 8, "Then said they unto him, Tell us, we pray thee, for whose cause this evil is upon us; What is thine occupation? and whence comest thou? what is thy country? and of what people art thou?"], [1, 9, "And he said unto them, I am an Hebrew; and I fear the LORD, the God of heaven, which hath made the sea and the dry land."], [1, 10, "Then were the men exceedingly afraid, and said unto him, Why hast thou done this? For the men knew that he fled from the presence of the LORD, because he had told them."], [1, 11, "Then said they unto him, What shall we do unto thee, that the sea may be calm unto us? for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous."], [1, 12, "And he said unto them, Take me up, and cast me forth into the sea; so shall the sea be calm unto you: for I know that for my sake this great tempest is upon you."], [1, 13, "Nevertheless the men rowed hard to bring it to the land; but they could not: for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous against them."], [1, 14, "Wherefore they cried unto the LORD, and said, We beseech thee, O LORD, we beseech thee, let us not perish for this man’s life, and lay not upon us innocent blood: for thou, O LORD, hast done as it pleased thee."], [1, 15, "So they took up Jonah, and cast him forth into the sea: and the sea ceased from her raging."], [1, 16, "Then the men feared the LORD exceedingly, and offered a sacrifice unto the LORD, and made vows."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "The sailors prayed while Jonah slept (verses 5–6). What do you notice about how the sailors act compared with Jonah?"}, {"r": "Jonah 1:1–17", "v": [[1, 1, "Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai, saying,"], [1, 2, "Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me."], [1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 4, "But the LORD sent out a great wind into the sea, and there was a mighty tempest in the sea, so that the ship was like to be broken."], [1, 5, "Then the mariners were afraid, and cried every man unto his god, and cast forth the wares that were in the ship into the sea, to lighten it of them. But Jonah was gone down into the sides of the ship; and he lay, and was fast asleep."], [1, 6, "So the shipmaster came to him, and said unto him, What meanest thou, O sleeper? arise, call upon thy God, if so be that God will think upon us, that we perish not."], [1, 7, "And they said every one to his fellow, Come, and let us cast lots, that we may know for whose cause this evil is upon us. So they cast lots, and the lot fell upon Jonah."], [1, 8, "Then said they unto him, Tell us, we pray thee, for whose cause this evil is upon us; What is thine occupation? and whence comest thou? what is thy country? and of what people art thou?"], [1, 9, "And he said unto them, I am an Hebrew; and I fear the LORD, the God of heaven, which hath made the sea and the dry land."], [1, 10, "Then were the men exceedingly afraid, and said unto him, Why hast thou done this? For the men knew that he fled from the presence of the LORD, because he had told them."], [1, 11, "Then said they unto him, What shall we do unto thee, that the sea may be calm unto us? for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous."], [1, 12, "And he said unto them, Take me up, and cast me forth into the sea; so shall the sea be calm unto you: for I know that for my sake this great tempest is upon you."], [1, 13, "Nevertheless the men rowed hard to bring it to the land; but they could not: for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous against them."], [1, 14, "Wherefore they cried unto the LORD, and said, We beseech thee, O LORD, we beseech thee, let us not perish for this man’s life, and lay not upon us innocent blood: for thou, O LORD, hast done as it pleased thee."], [1, 15, "So they took up Jonah, and cast him forth into the sea: and the sea ceased from her raging."], [1, 16, "Then the men feared the LORD exceedingly, and offered a sacrifice unto the LORD, and made vows."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "Jonah “rose up to flee… from the presence of the LORD” (verse 3). Why do we sometimes try to run from what God asks?"}], "ck": "Nineveh was a big city full of people who were enemies of Israel. God asked Jonah to go warn them.", "cf": "Nineveh was the capital of Assyria, Israel's feared and violent enemy. Instead of heading east, Jonah went to the port of Joppa and sailed for Tarshish, probably in Spain, about as far the other way as he could go.", "wd": ["great fish", "דָּג גָּדוֹל", "dag gadol", "Hebrew", "simply “a great fish.” The Hebrew doesn't say what kind; “whale” comes from later translations."]}, {"d": "2026-11-28", "w": 48, "t": "A second chance", "a": [{"r": "Jonah 2:1; 3:5", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."]], "q": "Jonah prayed inside the fish, and God heard him. Can God hear our prayers anywhere?", "act": "Whisper a short prayer together, then talk about how God hears even quiet prayers."}, {"r": "Jonah 2:1–2, 10; 3:1–5", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [2, 2, "And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou heardest my voice."], [2, 10, "And the LORD spake unto the fish, and it vomited out Jonah upon the dry land."], [3, 1, "And the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time, saying,"], [3, 2, "Arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid thee."], [3, 3, "So Jonah arose, and went unto Nineveh, according to the word of the LORD. Now Nineveh was an exceeding great city of three days’ journey."], [3, 4, "And Jonah began to enter into the city a day’s journey, and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown."], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."]], "q": "God gave Jonah a second chance (3:1). How did the people of Nineveh use their second chance?"}, {"r": "Jonah 2:1–10; 3:1–5, 10", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [2, 2, "And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou heardest my voice."], [2, 3, "For thou hadst cast me into the deep, in the midst of the seas; and the floods compassed me about: all thy billows and thy waves passed over me."], [2, 4, "Then I said, I am cast out of thy sight; yet I will look again toward thy holy temple."], [2, 5, "The waters compassed me about, even to the soul: the depth closed me round about, the weeds were wrapped about my head."], [2, 6, "I went down to the bottoms of the mountains; the earth with her bars was about me for ever: yet hast thou brought up my life from corruption, O LORD my God."], [2, 7, "When my soul fainted within me I remembered the LORD: and my prayer came in unto thee, into thine holy temple."], [2, 8, "They that observe lying vanities forsake their own mercy."], [2, 9, "But I will sacrifice unto thee with the voice of thanksgiving; I will pay that that I have vowed. Salvation is of the LORD."], [2, 10, "And the LORD spake unto the fish, and it vomited out Jonah upon the dry land."], [3, 1, "And the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time, saying,"], [3, 2, "Arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid thee."], [3, 3, "So Jonah arose, and went unto Nineveh, according to the word of the LORD. Now Nineveh was an exceeding great city of three days’ journey."], [3, 4, "And Jonah began to enter into the city a day’s journey, and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown."], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."], [3, 10, "And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not."]], "q": "“The word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time” (3:1). What does this story teach about second chances?"}, {"r": "Jonah 2:1–10; 3:1–10", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [2, 2, "And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou heardest my voice."], [2, 3, "For thou hadst cast me into the deep, in the midst of the seas; and the floods compassed me about: all thy billows and thy waves passed over me."], [2, 4, "Then I said, I am cast out of thy sight; yet I will look again toward thy holy temple."], [2, 5, "The waters compassed me about, even to the soul: the depth closed me round about, the weeds were wrapped about my head."], [2, 6, "I went down to the bottoms of the mountains; the earth with her bars was about me for ever: yet hast thou brought up my life from corruption, O LORD my God."], [2, 7, "When my soul fainted within me I remembered the LORD: and my prayer came in unto thee, into thine holy temple."], [2, 8, "They that observe lying vanities forsake their own mercy."], [2, 9, "But I will sacrifice unto thee with the voice of thanksgiving; I will pay that that I have vowed. Salvation is of the LORD."], [2, 10, "And the LORD spake unto the fish, and it vomited out Jonah upon the dry land."], [3, 1, "And the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time, saying,"], [3, 2, "Arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid thee."], [3, 3, "So Jonah arose, and went unto Nineveh, according to the word of the LORD. Now Nineveh was an exceeding great city of three days’ journey."], [3, 4, "And Jonah began to enter into the city a day’s journey, and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown."], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."], [3, 6, "For word came unto the king of Nineveh, and he arose from his throne, and he laid his robe from him, and covered him with sackcloth, and sat in ashes."], [3, 7, "And he caused it to be proclaimed and published through Nineveh by the decree of the king and his nobles, saying, Let neither man nor beast, herd nor flock, taste any thing: let them not feed, nor drink water:"], [3, 8, "But let man and beast be covered with sackcloth, and cry mightily unto God: yea, let them turn every one from his evil way, and from the violence that is in their hands."], [3, 9, "Who can tell if God will turn and repent, and turn away from his fierce anger, that we perish not?"], [3, 10, "And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not."]], "q": "Jonah remembered the Lord “when my soul fainted within me” (2:7). What does his prayer teach about turning to God when we're at our lowest?"}], "ck": "Jonah was inside the fish for three days. Then he finally went to Nineveh, and the whole city said sorry to God.", "cf": "Jesus called this “the sign of the prophet Jonas”: three days and nights in the fish pointed to His own death and Resurrection (Matthew 12:39–41). Nineveh's repentance is total, from the king down to the animals.", "wd": ["sackcloth", "שַׂק", "saq", "Hebrew", "rough cloth made of goat hair, worn to show sorrow and humility."]}, {"d": "2026-11-29", "w": 48, "t": "God's mercy for everyone", "a": [{"r": "Jonah 4:2", "v": [[4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."]], "q": "Jonah said God is “gracious… and merciful… and of great kindness.” How has God been kind to our family?", "act": "Make a list together of ways God has been kind to your family this week."}, {"r": "Jonah 4:1–4", "v": [[4, 1, "But it displeased Jonah exceedingly, and he was very angry."], [4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."], [4, 3, "Therefore now, O LORD, take, I beseech thee, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 4, "Then said the LORD, Doest thou well to be angry?"]], "q": "Jonah was angry that God forgave Nineveh. Why can it be hard to be happy when someone else is forgiven?"}, {"r": "Jonah 4:1–11", "v": [[4, 1, "But it displeased Jonah exceedingly, and he was very angry."], [4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."], [4, 3, "Therefore now, O LORD, take, I beseech thee, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 4, "Then said the LORD, Doest thou well to be angry?"], [4, 5, "So Jonah went out of the city, and sat on the east side of the city, and there made him a booth, and sat under it in the shadow, till he might see what would become of the city."], [4, 6, "And the LORD God prepared a gourd, and made it to come up over Jonah, that it might be a shadow over his head, to deliver him from his grief. So Jonah was exceeding glad of the gourd."], [4, 7, "But God prepared a worm when the morning rose the next day, and it smote the gourd that it withered."], [4, 8, "And it came to pass, when the sun did arise, that God prepared a vehement east wind; and the sun beat upon the head of Jonah, that he fainted, and wished in himself to die, and said, It is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 9, "And God said to Jonah, Doest thou well to be angry for the gourd? And he said, I do well to be angry, even unto death."], [4, 10, "Then said the LORD, Thou hast had pity on the gourd, for the which thou hast not laboured, neither madest it grow; which came up in a night, and perished in a night:"], [4, 11, "And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?"]], "q": "God used a plant to teach Jonah (verses 6–11). What was the lesson?"}, {"r": "Jonah 4:1–11", "v": [[4, 1, "But it displeased Jonah exceedingly, and he was very angry."], [4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."], [4, 3, "Therefore now, O LORD, take, I beseech thee, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 4, "Then said the LORD, Doest thou well to be angry?"], [4, 5, "So Jonah went out of the city, and sat on the east side of the city, and there made him a booth, and sat under it in the shadow, till he might see what would become of the city."], [4, 6, "And the LORD God prepared a gourd, and made it to come up over Jonah, that it might be a shadow over his head, to deliver him from his grief. So Jonah was exceeding glad of the gourd."], [4, 7, "But God prepared a worm when the morning rose the next day, and it smote the gourd that it withered."], [4, 8, "And it came to pass, when the sun did arise, that God prepared a vehement east wind; and the sun beat upon the head of Jonah, that he fainted, and wished in himself to die, and said, It is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 9, "And God said to Jonah, Doest thou well to be angry for the gourd? And he said, I do well to be angry, even unto death."], [4, 10, "Then said the LORD, Thou hast had pity on the gourd, for the which thou hast not laboured, neither madest it grow; which came up in a night, and perished in a night:"], [4, 11, "And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?"]], "q": "The book ends with God's question (verse 11). How would you answer it, and who might be our Nineveh?"}], "ck": "Jonah was upset that God forgave Nineveh. God used a plant to teach him a lesson.", "cf": "The book ends with a question, not an answer. God points to 120,000 people who “cannot discern between their right hand and their left,” likely meaning children or people who didn't know better.", "wd": ["gourd", "קִיקָיוֹן", "qiqayon", "Hebrew", "probably a castor-oil plant, a fast-growing plant with big leaves that gives quick shade."]}, {"d": "2026-11-30", "w": 49, "t": "What God asks of us", "a": [{"r": "Micah 6:8", "v": [[6, 8, "He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?"]], "q": "God asks us to be fair, to be kind and to walk with Him. Which one can you do today?", "act": "Take a short “walk humbly with God” around the house, and at each room name one kind thing you could do there."}, {"r": "Micah 6:6–8", "v": [[6, 6, "Wherewith shall I come before the LORD, and bow myself before the high God? shall I come before him with burnt offerings, with calves of a year old?"], [6, 7, "Will the LORD be pleased with thousands of rams, or with ten thousands of rivers of oil? shall I give my firstborn for my transgression, the fruit of my body for the sin of my soul?"], [6, 8, "He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?"]], "q": "The people asked if God wanted more gifts and sacrifices. What does verse 8 say He wants instead?"}, {"r": "Micah 6:6–8", "v": [[6, 6, "Wherewith shall I come before the LORD, and bow myself before the high God? shall I come before him with burnt offerings, with calves of a year old?"], [6, 7, "Will the LORD be pleased with thousands of rams, or with ten thousands of rivers of oil? shall I give my firstborn for my transgression, the fruit of my body for the sin of my soul?"], [6, 8, "He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?"]], "q": "Verse 7 offers bigger and bigger sacrifices. Why do you think God's answer in verse 8 is so simple, and is it easier or harder?"}, {"r": "Micah 6:1–8", "v": [[6, 1, "Hear ye now what the LORD saith; Arise, contend thou before the mountains, and let the hills hear thy voice."], [6, 2, "Hear ye, O mountains, the LORD’s controversy, and ye strong foundations of the earth: for the LORD hath a controversy with his people, and he will plead with Israel."], [6, 3, "O my people, what have I done unto thee? and wherein have I wearied thee? testify against me."], [6, 4, "For I brought thee up out of the land of Egypt, and redeemed thee out of the house of servants; and I sent before thee Moses, Aaron, and Miriam."], [6, 5, "O my people, remember now what Balak king of Moab consulted, and what Balaam the son of Beor answered him from Shittim unto Gilgal; that ye may know the righteousness of the LORD."], [6, 6, "Wherewith shall I come before the LORD, and bow myself before the high God? shall I come before him with burnt offerings, with calves of a year old?"], [6, 7, "Will the LORD be pleased with thousands of rams, or with ten thousands of rivers of oil? shall I give my firstborn for my transgression, the fruit of my body for the sin of my soul?"], [6, 8, "He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?"]], "q": "God opens with a court case: “O my people, what have I done unto thee?” (verse 3). How does remembering what He has done shape what He asks?"}], "ck": "Micah was a prophet who lived in the country, far from the big city. He told people what God really wants from them.", "cf": "Micah came from the small town of Moresheth, about 25 miles southwest of Jerusalem, around 740–700 BC, the same time as Isaiah. Chapter 6 is staged like a trial, with the mountains as witnesses.", "wd": ["humbly", "הַצְנֵעַ", "hatsnea", "Hebrew", "carefully, modestly. To “walk humbly” is to walk attentively beside God, not ahead of Him."]}, {"d": "2026-12-01", "w": 49, "t": "A Savior from Bethlehem", "a": [{"r": "Micah 5:2", "v": [[5, 2, "But thou, Beth-lehem Ephratah, though thou be little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of thee shall he come forth unto me that is to be ruler in Israel; whose goings forth have been from of old, from everlasting."]], "q": "Long before Jesus was born, Micah said He would come from a little town called Bethlehem. Do you know who was born there?", "act": "Make a little town out of blocks or cups, and put a star on top of the smallest house."}, {"r": "Micah 5:2", "v": [[5, 2, "But thou, Beth-lehem Ephratah, though thou be little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of thee shall he come forth unto me that is to be ruler in Israel; whose goings forth have been from of old, from everlasting."]], "q": "Bethlehem was “little among the thousands of Judah.” Why might God choose a small, ordinary place for His Son?"}, {"r": "Micah 5:2–4", "v": [[5, 2, "But thou, Beth-lehem Ephratah, though thou be little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of thee shall he come forth unto me that is to be ruler in Israel; whose goings forth have been from of old, from everlasting."], [5, 3, "Therefore will he give them up, until the time that she which travaileth hath brought forth: then the remnant of his brethren shall return unto the children of Israel."], [5, 4, "And he shall stand and feed in the strength of the LORD, in the majesty of the name of the LORD his God; and they shall abide: for now shall he be great unto the ends of the earth."]], "q": "Verse 2 says His “goings forth have been from of old, from everlasting.” What does that tell you about who Jesus was before He was born?"}, {"r": "Micah 5:2–5", "v": [[5, 2, "But thou, Beth-lehem Ephratah, though thou be little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of thee shall he come forth unto me that is to be ruler in Israel; whose goings forth have been from of old, from everlasting."], [5, 3, "Therefore will he give them up, until the time that she which travaileth hath brought forth: then the remnant of his brethren shall return unto the children of Israel."], [5, 4, "And he shall stand and feed in the strength of the LORD, in the majesty of the name of the LORD his God; and they shall abide: for now shall he be great unto the ends of the earth."], [5, 5, "And this man shall be the peace, when the Assyrian shall come into our land: and when he shall tread in our palaces, then shall we raise against him seven shepherds, and eight principal men."]], "q": "When the wise men asked where the King would be born, the chief priests quoted this verse (Matthew 2:5–6). What does verse 5, “this man shall be the peace,” mean to you?"}], "ck": "Bethlehem was a small town near Jerusalem. King David grew up there as a shepherd boy.", "cf": "Bethlehem, about six miles south of Jerusalem, was David's hometown. Micah wrote some 700 years before Jesus was born, yet the chief priests in Herod's day knew this verse pointed to where the Messiah would come from.", "wd": ["Bethlehem", "בֵּית לֶחֶם", "Beit Lechem", "Hebrew", "“house of bread.” Jesus, born there, later called Himself “the bread of life” (John 6:35)."]}, {"d": "2026-12-02", "w": 49, "t": "Swords into plowshares", "a": [{"r": "Micah 4:3", "v": [[4, 3, "And he shall judge among many people, and rebuke strong nations afar off; and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up a sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more."]], "q": "Someday people will turn swords into tools for farming and stop fighting. How can we make peace in our home?", "act": "Turn a toy or a stick “sword” into a garden tool, and talk about choosing peace."}, {"r": "Micah 4:1–3", "v": [[4, 1, "But in the last days it shall come to pass, that the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established in the top of the mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills; and people shall flow unto it."], [4, 2, "And many nations shall come, and say, Come, and let us go up to the mountain of the LORD, and to the house of the God of Jacob; and he will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths: for the law shall go forth of Zion, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem."], [4, 3, "And he shall judge among many people, and rebuke strong nations afar off; and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up a sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more."]], "q": "People will come to “the mountain of the LORD” to learn His ways. Where do we go to learn His ways?"}, {"r": "Micah 4:1–4", "v": [[4, 1, "But in the last days it shall come to pass, that the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established in the top of the mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills; and people shall flow unto it."], [4, 2, "And many nations shall come, and say, Come, and let us go up to the mountain of the LORD, and to the house of the God of Jacob; and he will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths: for the law shall go forth of Zion, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem."], [4, 3, "And he shall judge among many people, and rebuke strong nations afar off; and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up a sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more."], [4, 4, "But they shall sit every man under his vine and under his fig tree; and none shall make them afraid: for the mouth of the LORD of hosts hath spoken it."]], "q": "Verse 4 pictures everyone sitting under their own vine and fig tree, unafraid. What makes a place feel that safe?"}, {"r": "Micah 4:1–5", "v": [[4, 1, "But in the last days it shall come to pass, that the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established in the top of the mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills; and people shall flow unto it."], [4, 2, "And many nations shall come, and say, Come, and let us go up to the mountain of the LORD, and to the house of the God of Jacob; and he will teach us of his ways, and we will walk in his paths: for the law shall go forth of Zion, and the word of the LORD from Jerusalem."], [4, 3, "And he shall judge among many people, and rebuke strong nations afar off; and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks: nation shall not lift up a sword against nation, neither shall they learn war any more."], [4, 4, "But they shall sit every man under his vine and under his fig tree; and none shall make them afraid: for the mouth of the LORD of hosts hath spoken it."], [4, 5, "For all people will walk every one in the name of his god, and we will walk in the name of the LORD our God for ever and ever."]], "q": "This prophecy also appears in Isaiah 2:2–4, and Latter-day Saints see the temple in “the mountain of the LORD's house.” How does the temple teach us peace?"}], "ck": "Long ago soldiers used swords and spears. Micah said a day will come when people turn them into farm tools.", "cf": "A plowshare is the metal blade that cuts the soil. Melting weapons into farm tools means the world no longer needs war. The same words appear in Isaiah 2:2–4, and Latter-day Saints often connect “the mountain of the LORD's house” with temples.", "wd": ["plowshares", "אִתִּים", "ittim", "Hebrew", "the iron blades of a plow. Beating a sword into one means turning a tool of death into a tool that grows food."]}, {"d": "2026-12-03", "w": 49, "t": "He delights in mercy", "a": [{"r": "Micah 7:18–19", "v": [[7, 18, "Who is a God like unto thee, that pardoneth iniquity, and passeth by the transgression of the remnant of his heritage? he retaineth not his anger for ever, because he delighteth in mercy."], [7, 19, "He will turn again, he will have compassion upon us; he will subdue our iniquities; and thou wilt cast all their sins into the depths of the sea."]], "q": "God loves to forgive us. Micah says He throws our sins into the deep sea. Can you get something back from the bottom of the ocean?", "act": "Drop a pebble into a bowl of water and talk about how God takes our sins away when we repent."}, {"r": "Micah 7:18–19", "v": [[7, 18, "Who is a God like unto thee, that pardoneth iniquity, and passeth by the transgression of the remnant of his heritage? he retaineth not his anger for ever, because he delighteth in mercy."], [7, 19, "He will turn again, he will have compassion upon us; he will subdue our iniquities; and thou wilt cast all their sins into the depths of the sea."]], "q": "Micah says God “delighteth in mercy.” Delight means He enjoys it. How does that change how you feel about repenting?"}, {"r": "Micah 7:18–20", "v": [[7, 18, "Who is a God like unto thee, that pardoneth iniquity, and passeth by the transgression of the remnant of his heritage? he retaineth not his anger for ever, because he delighteth in mercy."], [7, 19, "He will turn again, he will have compassion upon us; he will subdue our iniquities; and thou wilt cast all their sins into the depths of the sea."], [7, 20, "Thou wilt perform the truth to Jacob, and the mercy to Abraham, which thou hast sworn unto our fathers from the days of old."]], "q": "Verse 19 says He will “subdue our iniquities.” How is that different from just forgiving them?"}, {"r": "Micah 7:7–9, 18–20", "v": [[7, 7, "Therefore I will look unto the LORD; I will wait for the God of my salvation: my God will hear me."], [7, 8, "Rejoice not against me, O mine enemy: when I fall, I shall arise; when I sit in darkness, the LORD shall be a light unto me."], [7, 9, "I will bear the indignation of the LORD, because I have sinned against him, until he plead my cause, and execute judgment for me: he will bring me forth to the light, and I shall behold his righteousness."], [7, 18, "Who is a God like unto thee, that pardoneth iniquity, and passeth by the transgression of the remnant of his heritage? he retaineth not his anger for ever, because he delighteth in mercy."], [7, 19, "He will turn again, he will have compassion upon us; he will subdue our iniquities; and thou wilt cast all their sins into the depths of the sea."], [7, 20, "Thou wilt perform the truth to Jacob, and the mercy to Abraham, which thou hast sworn unto our fathers from the days of old."]], "q": "Micah's name means “Who is like the LORD?” and verse 18 asks that same question. How would you answer it after reading these verses?"}], "ck": "Micah asked, “Who is a God like unto thee?” Nobody forgives like God does.", "cf": "Micah ends his book with a wordplay on his own name, Mikah, “Who is like the LORD?” His answer: a God who pardons and casts sins into the depths of the sea.", "wd": ["pardoneth", "נֹשֵׂא", "nose", "Hebrew", "literally “lifts up” or “carries.” God doesn't just excuse sin; He lifts it off and carries it away."]}, {"d": "2026-12-04", "w": 49, "t": "A strong hold in trouble", "a": [{"r": "Nahum 1:7", "v": [[1, 7, "The LORD is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble; and he knoweth them that trust in him."]], "q": "The Lord is like a strong castle we can run to when we're scared. When have you felt safe with Heavenly Father?", "act": "Build a fort with pillows or blankets, climb in together and say a short prayer of thanks."}, {"r": "Nahum 1:3, 7", "v": [[1, 3, "The LORD is slow to anger, and great in power, and will not at all acquit the wicked: the LORD hath his way in the whirlwind and in the storm, and the clouds are the dust of his feet."], [1, 7, "The LORD is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble; and he knoweth them that trust in him."]], "q": "God is powerful, and He is also good to people who trust Him. Why do we need Him to be both?"}, {"r": "Nahum 1:2–7", "v": [[1, 2, "God is jealous, and the LORD revengeth; the LORD revengeth, and is furious; the LORD will take vengeance on his adversaries, and he reserveth wrath for his enemies."], [1, 3, "The LORD is slow to anger, and great in power, and will not at all acquit the wicked: the LORD hath his way in the whirlwind and in the storm, and the clouds are the dust of his feet."], [1, 4, "He rebuketh the sea, and maketh it dry, and drieth up all the rivers: Bashan languisheth, and Carmel, and the flower of Lebanon languisheth."], [1, 5, "The mountains quake at him, and the hills melt, and the earth is burned at his presence, yea, the world, and all that dwell therein."], [1, 6, "Who can stand before his indignation? and who can abide in the fierceness of his anger? his fury is poured out like fire, and the rocks are thrown down by him."], [1, 7, "The LORD is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble; and he knoweth them that trust in him."]], "q": "Most of Nahum is about judgment on Nineveh, yet verse 7 is full of comfort. What does it mean that He “knoweth them that trust in him”?"}, {"r": "Nahum 1:2–8", "v": [[1, 2, "God is jealous, and the LORD revengeth; the LORD revengeth, and is furious; the LORD will take vengeance on his adversaries, and he reserveth wrath for his enemies."], [1, 3, "The LORD is slow to anger, and great in power, and will not at all acquit the wicked: the LORD hath his way in the whirlwind and in the storm, and the clouds are the dust of his feet."], [1, 4, "He rebuketh the sea, and maketh it dry, and drieth up all the rivers: Bashan languisheth, and Carmel, and the flower of Lebanon languisheth."], [1, 5, "The mountains quake at him, and the hills melt, and the earth is burned at his presence, yea, the world, and all that dwell therein."], [1, 6, "Who can stand before his indignation? and who can abide in the fierceness of his anger? his fury is poured out like fire, and the rocks are thrown down by him."], [1, 7, "The LORD is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble; and he knoweth them that trust in him."], [1, 8, "But with an overrunning flood he will make an utter end of the place thereof, and darkness shall pursue his enemies."]], "q": "Nahum shows God's anger at cruelty and His care for the trusting, side by side. How do you hold both together?"}], "ck": "Nahum warned the city of Nineveh. This was the same city that repented when Jonah came, but now it had turned wicked again.", "cf": "About a hundred years after Jonah, Assyria's capital Nineveh had become cruel again. Nahum foretold its fall, which came in 612 BC when the Babylonians and Medes destroyed it. His book brought comfort to people Assyria had hurt.", "wd": ["strong hold", "מָעוֹז", "ma'oz", "Hebrew", "a fortress or place of safety on high ground, where people ran when enemies came."]}, {"d": "2026-12-05", "w": 49, "t": "Wait for it", "a": [{"r": "Habakkuk 2:3", "v": [[2, 3, "For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry."]], "q": "Sometimes we have to wait for God's answers. What is something you had to wait for that was worth it?", "act": "Plant a seed or set out a treat for later, and talk about how waiting can be part of trusting God."}, {"r": "Habakkuk 1:2; 2:1–3", "v": [[1, 2, "O LORD, how long shall I cry, and thou wilt not hear! even cry out unto thee of violence, and thou wilt not save!"], [2, 1, "I will stand upon my watch, and set me upon the tower, and will watch to see what he will say unto me, and what I shall answer when I am reproved."], [2, 2, "And the LORD answered me, and said, Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it."], [2, 3, "For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry."]], "q": "Habakkuk asked God hard questions, then climbed a watchtower to wait for an answer. What can we do while we wait for answers?"}, {"r": "Habakkuk 1:2–4; 2:1–4", "v": [[1, 2, "O LORD, how long shall I cry, and thou wilt not hear! even cry out unto thee of violence, and thou wilt not save!"], [1, 3, "Why dost thou shew me iniquity, and cause me to behold grievance? for spoiling and violence are before me: and there are that raise up strife and contention."], [1, 4, "Therefore the law is slacked, and judgment doth never go forth: for the wicked doth compass about the righteous; therefore wrong judgment proceedeth."], [2, 1, "I will stand upon my watch, and set me upon the tower, and will watch to see what he will say unto me, and what I shall answer when I am reproved."], [2, 2, "And the LORD answered me, and said, Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it."], [2, 3, "For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry."], [2, 4, "Behold, his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him: but the just shall live by his faith."]], "q": "Habakkuk asked, “How long?” God answered, “Wait for it; because it will surely come.” Is it okay to ask God hard questions?"}, {"r": "Habakkuk 1:1–5; 2:1–4", "v": [[1, 1, "The burden which Habakkuk the prophet did see."], [1, 2, "O LORD, how long shall I cry, and thou wilt not hear! even cry out unto thee of violence, and thou wilt not save!"], [1, 3, "Why dost thou shew me iniquity, and cause me to behold grievance? for spoiling and violence are before me: and there are that raise up strife and contention."], [1, 4, "Therefore the law is slacked, and judgment doth never go forth: for the wicked doth compass about the righteous; therefore wrong judgment proceedeth."], [1, 5, "Behold ye among the heathen, and regard, and wonder marvellously: for I will work a work in your days, which ye will not believe, though it be told you."], [2, 1, "I will stand upon my watch, and set me upon the tower, and will watch to see what he will say unto me, and what I shall answer when I am reproved."], [2, 2, "And the LORD answered me, and said, Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it."], [2, 3, "For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry."], [2, 4, "Behold, his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him: but the just shall live by his faith."]], "q": "“The just shall live by his faith” (2:4) is quoted three times in the New Testament (Romans 1:17; Galatians 3:11; Hebrews 10:38). What does it mean to live by faith while answers are delayed?"}], "ck": "Habakkuk asked God why bad things were happening. God answered him, but told him he would have to wait.", "cf": "Habakkuk prophesied as Babylon was rising, around 600 BC. His book is a conversation: he complains, God answers, he complains again, and then he waits on the watchtower for the reply.", "wd": ["faith", "אֱמוּנָה", "emunah", "Hebrew", "faithfulness, steadiness. The word is related to “amen.” Faith here means holding steady and trusting."]}, {"d": "2026-12-06", "w": 49, "t": "God sings over you", "a": [{"r": "Zephaniah 3:17", "v": [[3, 17, "The LORD thy God in the midst of thee is mighty; he will save, he will rejoice over thee with joy; he will rest in his love, he will joy over thee with singing."]], "q": "This verse says God is so happy with His children that He sings over them. How does it feel to know God is happy about you?", "act": "Sing a favorite Primary song together, and say the name of each person in the family in it."}, {"r": "Zephaniah 3:14, 17", "v": [[3, 14, "Sing, O daughter of Zion; shout, O Israel; be glad and rejoice with all the heart, O daughter of Jerusalem."], [3, 17, "The LORD thy God in the midst of thee is mighty; he will save, he will rejoice over thee with joy; he will rest in his love, he will joy over thee with singing."]], "q": "God tells His people to sing and be glad, and then He rejoices over them too. What makes you want to sing?"}, {"r": "Zephaniah 3:14–17", "v": [[3, 14, "Sing, O daughter of Zion; shout, O Israel; be glad and rejoice with all the heart, O daughter of Jerusalem."], [3, 15, "The LORD hath taken away thy judgments, he hath cast out thine enemy: the king of Israel, even the LORD, is in the midst of thee: thou shalt not see evil any more."], [3, 16, "In that day it shall be said to Jerusalem, Fear thou not: and to Zion, Let not thine hands be slack."], [3, 17, "The LORD thy God in the midst of thee is mighty; he will save, he will rejoice over thee with joy; he will rest in his love, he will joy over thee with singing."]], "q": "Verse 17 says God “will rest in his love.” What do you think it means for God to be quiet and at peace in His love for you?"}, {"r": "Zephaniah 3:14–20", "v": [[3, 14, "Sing, O daughter of Zion; shout, O Israel; be glad and rejoice with all the heart, O daughter of Jerusalem."], [3, 15, "The LORD hath taken away thy judgments, he hath cast out thine enemy: the king of Israel, even the LORD, is in the midst of thee: thou shalt not see evil any more."], [3, 16, "In that day it shall be said to Jerusalem, Fear thou not: and to Zion, Let not thine hands be slack."], [3, 17, "The LORD thy God in the midst of thee is mighty; he will save, he will rejoice over thee with joy; he will rest in his love, he will joy over thee with singing."], [3, 18, "I will gather them that are sorrowful for the solemn assembly, who are of thee, to whom the reproach of it was a burden."], [3, 19, "Behold, at that time I will undo all that afflict thee: and I will save her that halteth, and gather her that was driven out; and I will get them praise and fame in every land where they have been put to shame."], [3, 20, "At that time will I bring you again, even in the time that I gather you: for I will make you a name and a praise among all people of the earth, when I turn back your captivity before your eyes, saith the LORD."]], "q": "Most of Zephaniah warns about “the day of the LORD,” but it ends here. What does it say about God that His last word is joy?"}], "ck": "Zephaniah warned people about the day of the Lord, but he ended his book with a happy promise.", "cf": "Zephaniah prophesied in the days of King Josiah, about 640–610 BC. His book moves from warning to one of the most joyful pictures of God in scripture: the Lord Himself singing over His people.", "wd": ["singing", "רִנָּה", "rinnah", "Hebrew", "a ringing shout of joy, the kind of singing that bursts out when you're very happy."]}, {"d": "2026-12-07", "w": 50, "t": "Consider your ways", "a": [{"r": "Haggai 1:5", "v": [[1, 5, "Now therefore thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways."]], "q": "Haggai told the people to think about how they were living. What is one good thing you do every day?", "act": "Draw a quick picture of your day and circle the part where you remember Heavenly Father."}, {"r": "Haggai 1:4–6", "v": [[1, 4, "Is it time for you, O ye, to dwell in your cieled houses, and this house lie waste?"], [1, 5, "Now therefore thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways."], [1, 6, "Ye have sown much, and bring in little; ye eat, but ye have not enough; ye drink, but ye are not filled with drink; ye clothe you, but there is none warm; and he that earneth wages earneth wages to put it into a bag with holes."]], "q": "The people built nice houses for themselves but left the Lord's house unfinished. What does it mean to put God first?"}, {"r": "Haggai 1:4–7", "v": [[1, 4, "Is it time for you, O ye, to dwell in your cieled houses, and this house lie waste?"], [1, 5, "Now therefore thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways."], [1, 6, "Ye have sown much, and bring in little; ye eat, but ye have not enough; ye drink, but ye are not filled with drink; ye clothe you, but there is none warm; and he that earneth wages earneth wages to put it into a bag with holes."], [1, 7, "Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways."]], "q": "Verse 6 says they earned wages and put them “into a bag with holes.” What things in life feel like that?"}, {"r": "Haggai 1:2–9", "v": [[1, 2, "Thus speaketh the LORD of hosts, saying, This people say, The time is not come, the time that the LORD’s house should be built."], [1, 3, "Then came the word of the LORD by Haggai the prophet, saying,"], [1, 4, "Is it time for you, O ye, to dwell in your cieled houses, and this house lie waste?"], [1, 5, "Now therefore thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways."], [1, 6, "Ye have sown much, and bring in little; ye eat, but ye have not enough; ye drink, but ye are not filled with drink; ye clothe you, but there is none warm; and he that earneth wages earneth wages to put it into a bag with holes."], [1, 7, "Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Consider your ways."], [1, 8, "Go up to the mountain, and bring wood, and build the house; and I will take pleasure in it, and I will be glorified, saith the LORD."], [1, 9, "Ye looked for much, and, lo, it came to little; and when ye brought it home, I did blow upon it. Why? saith the LORD of hosts. Because of mine house that is waste, and ye run every man unto his own house."]], "q": "“Consider your ways” comes twice (verses 5 and 7). If you looked honestly at your week, what would show what you're putting first?"}], "ck": "After many years away, some of God's people came home to Jerusalem. They started building God's house, the temple, but then they stopped.", "cf": "In 520 BC, about 18 years after Cyrus let the Jews return, the foundation of the temple had been laid but the work had stalled. Haggai's four short messages, all dated, got the people building again.", "wd": ["consider", "שִׂימוּ לְבַבְכֶם", "simu levavkhem", "Hebrew", "literally “set your heart on.” Think deeply and take it to heart."]}, {"d": "2026-12-08", "w": 50, "t": "The Lord stirred them up", "a": [{"r": "Haggai 1:8, 14", "v": [[1, 8, "Go up to the mountain, and bring wood, and build the house; and I will take pleasure in it, and I will be glorified, saith the LORD."], [1, 14, "And the LORD stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and the spirit of Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest, and the spirit of all the remnant of the people; and they came and did work in the house of the LORD of hosts, their God,"]], "q": "The people went to the mountain to get wood to build the temple. What can you do to help with God's work?", "act": "Gather a few blocks or sticks and build a little “temple” together while you talk about real temples."}, {"r": "Haggai 1:12–14", "v": [[1, 12, "Then Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, and Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest, with all the remnant of the people, obeyed the voice of the LORD their God, and the words of Haggai the prophet, as the LORD their God had sent him, and the people did fear before the LORD."], [1, 13, "Then spake Haggai the LORD’s messenger in the LORD’s message unto the people, saying, I am with you, saith the LORD."], [1, 14, "And the LORD stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and the spirit of Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest, and the spirit of all the remnant of the people; and they came and did work in the house of the LORD of hosts, their God,"]], "q": "When the people obeyed, God “stirred up” their spirits. What helps you feel excited to do good things?"}, {"r": "Haggai 1:12–14", "v": [[1, 12, "Then Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, and Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest, with all the remnant of the people, obeyed the voice of the LORD their God, and the words of Haggai the prophet, as the LORD their God had sent him, and the people did fear before the LORD."], [1, 13, "Then spake Haggai the LORD’s messenger in the LORD’s message unto the people, saying, I am with you, saith the LORD."], [1, 14, "And the LORD stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and the spirit of Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest, and the spirit of all the remnant of the people; and they came and did work in the house of the LORD of hosts, their God,"]], "q": "Verse 13 is God's whole message: “I am with you.” Why might those four words be enough to get people working again?"}, {"r": "Haggai 1:12–15", "v": [[1, 12, "Then Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, and Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest, with all the remnant of the people, obeyed the voice of the LORD their God, and the words of Haggai the prophet, as the LORD their God had sent him, and the people did fear before the LORD."], [1, 13, "Then spake Haggai the LORD’s messenger in the LORD’s message unto the people, saying, I am with you, saith the LORD."], [1, 14, "And the LORD stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel the son of Shealtiel, governor of Judah, and the spirit of Joshua the son of Josedech, the high priest, and the spirit of all the remnant of the people; and they came and did work in the house of the LORD of hosts, their God,"], [1, 15, "In the four and twentieth day of the sixth month, in the second year of Darius the king."]], "q": "Leaders, priest and people all obeyed together, and within weeks the work restarted (verse 15). What makes a family or ward move together like that?"}], "ck": "When the people listened to Haggai, God helped them feel happy and ready to work on the temple again.", "cf": "Zerubbabel the governor, Joshua the high priest and the people all responded, and the work began again on the 24th day of that same month, about three weeks after Haggai's first message (1:1, 15).", "wd": ["stirred up", "עוּר", "ur", "Hebrew", "to wake up or rouse, like waking someone from sleep."]}, {"d": "2026-12-09", "w": 50, "t": "Be strong, for I am with you", "a": [{"r": "Haggai 2:4–5", "v": [[2, 4, "Yet now be strong, O Zerubbabel, saith the LORD; and be strong, O Joshua, son of Josedech, the high priest; and be strong, all ye people of the land, saith the LORD, and work: for I am with you, saith the LORD of hosts:"], [2, 5, "According to the word that I covenanted with you when ye came out of Egypt, so my spirit remaineth among you: fear ye not."]], "q": "God told His people three times, “Be strong.” Then He said, “I am with you.” When do you need to be strong?", "act": "Have everyone flex their muscles and say, “Be strong, for God is with me!”"}, {"r": "Haggai 2:3–5", "v": [[2, 3, "Who is left among you that saw this house in her first glory? and how do ye see it now? is it not in your eyes in comparison of it as nothing?"], [2, 4, "Yet now be strong, O Zerubbabel, saith the LORD; and be strong, O Joshua, son of Josedech, the high priest; and be strong, all ye people of the land, saith the LORD, and work: for I am with you, saith the LORD of hosts:"], [2, 5, "According to the word that I covenanted with you when ye came out of Egypt, so my spirit remaineth among you: fear ye not."]], "q": "Some older people cried because the new temple looked smaller than Solomon's. What did God promise them?"}, {"r": "Haggai 2:3–9", "v": [[2, 3, "Who is left among you that saw this house in her first glory? and how do ye see it now? is it not in your eyes in comparison of it as nothing?"], [2, 4, "Yet now be strong, O Zerubbabel, saith the LORD; and be strong, O Joshua, son of Josedech, the high priest; and be strong, all ye people of the land, saith the LORD, and work: for I am with you, saith the LORD of hosts:"], [2, 5, "According to the word that I covenanted with you when ye came out of Egypt, so my spirit remaineth among you: fear ye not."], [2, 6, "For thus saith the LORD of hosts; Yet once, it is a little while, and I will shake the heavens, and the earth, and the sea, and the dry land;"], [2, 7, "And I will shake all nations, and the desire of all nations shall come: and I will fill this house with glory, saith the LORD of hosts."], [2, 8, "The silver is mine, and the gold is mine, saith the LORD of hosts."], [2, 9, "The glory of this latter house shall be greater than of the former, saith the LORD of hosts: and in this place will I give peace, saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "Verse 9 says the glory of this house would be greater than the first. How could a smaller building have greater glory?"}, {"r": "Haggai 2:3–9", "v": [[2, 3, "Who is left among you that saw this house in her first glory? and how do ye see it now? is it not in your eyes in comparison of it as nothing?"], [2, 4, "Yet now be strong, O Zerubbabel, saith the LORD; and be strong, O Joshua, son of Josedech, the high priest; and be strong, all ye people of the land, saith the LORD, and work: for I am with you, saith the LORD of hosts:"], [2, 5, "According to the word that I covenanted with you when ye came out of Egypt, so my spirit remaineth among you: fear ye not."], [2, 6, "For thus saith the LORD of hosts; Yet once, it is a little while, and I will shake the heavens, and the earth, and the sea, and the dry land;"], [2, 7, "And I will shake all nations, and the desire of all nations shall come: and I will fill this house with glory, saith the LORD of hosts."], [2, 8, "The silver is mine, and the gold is mine, saith the LORD of hosts."], [2, 9, "The glory of this latter house shall be greater than of the former, saith the LORD of hosts: and in this place will I give peace, saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "This temple was later rebuilt by Herod, and Jesus taught in it. How does that help explain the promise of greater glory and of peace in “this place”?"}], "ck": "The new temple wasn't as fancy as the old one, and some people were sad. God told them not to worry.", "cf": "Older returnees remembered Solomon's temple, destroyed in 586 BC, and the new one looked like “nothing” in comparison (2:3). God promised to fill it with glory. It stood, later enlarged by Herod, until AD 70.", "wd": ["be strong", "חֲזַק", "chazaq", "Hebrew", "be firm, hold tight, take courage. God told Joshua the same thing as he entered the promised land (Joshua 1:6)."]}, {"d": "2026-12-10", "w": 50, "t": "Clean clothes from God", "a": [{"r": "Zechariah 3:3–4", "v": [[3, 3, "Now Joshua was clothed with filthy garments, and stood before the angel."], [3, 4, "And he answered and spake unto those that stood before him, saying, Take away the filthy garments from him. And unto him he said, Behold, I have caused thine iniquity to pass from thee, and I will clothe thee with change of raiment."]], "q": "In a vision, a man in dirty clothes got clean new ones. Jesus can make us clean when we repent. How do you feel in clean clothes?", "act": "Change into clean pajamas or wash hands together, and talk about how repenting makes us clean inside."}, {"r": "Zechariah 3:1–5", "v": [[3, 1, "And he shewed me Joshua the high priest standing before the angel of the LORD, and Satan standing at his right hand to resist him."], [3, 2, "And the LORD said unto Satan, The LORD rebuke thee, O Satan; even the LORD that hath chosen Jerusalem rebuke thee: is not this a brand plucked out of the fire?"], [3, 3, "Now Joshua was clothed with filthy garments, and stood before the angel."], [3, 4, "And he answered and spake unto those that stood before him, saying, Take away the filthy garments from him. And unto him he said, Behold, I have caused thine iniquity to pass from thee, and I will clothe thee with change of raiment."], [3, 5, "And I said, Let them set a fair mitre upon his head. So they set a fair mitre upon his head, and clothed him with garments. And the angel of the LORD stood by."]], "q": "Satan accused Joshua, but the Lord took away his filthy clothes. Who is stronger, Satan or the Lord?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 3:1–7", "v": [[3, 1, "And he shewed me Joshua the high priest standing before the angel of the LORD, and Satan standing at his right hand to resist him."], [3, 2, "And the LORD said unto Satan, The LORD rebuke thee, O Satan; even the LORD that hath chosen Jerusalem rebuke thee: is not this a brand plucked out of the fire?"], [3, 3, "Now Joshua was clothed with filthy garments, and stood before the angel."], [3, 4, "And he answered and spake unto those that stood before him, saying, Take away the filthy garments from him. And unto him he said, Behold, I have caused thine iniquity to pass from thee, and I will clothe thee with change of raiment."], [3, 5, "And I said, Let them set a fair mitre upon his head. So they set a fair mitre upon his head, and clothed him with garments. And the angel of the LORD stood by."], [3, 6, "And the angel of the LORD protested unto Joshua, saying,"], [3, 7, "Thus saith the LORD of hosts; If thou wilt walk in my ways, and if thou wilt keep my charge, then thou shalt also judge my house, and shalt also keep my courts, and I will give thee places to walk among these that stand by."]], "q": "Joshua didn't clean himself; the clothes were taken off and new ones put on. What does that show about who makes us clean?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 3:1–7", "v": [[3, 1, "And he shewed me Joshua the high priest standing before the angel of the LORD, and Satan standing at his right hand to resist him."], [3, 2, "And the LORD said unto Satan, The LORD rebuke thee, O Satan; even the LORD that hath chosen Jerusalem rebuke thee: is not this a brand plucked out of the fire?"], [3, 3, "Now Joshua was clothed with filthy garments, and stood before the angel."], [3, 4, "And he answered and spake unto those that stood before him, saying, Take away the filthy garments from him. And unto him he said, Behold, I have caused thine iniquity to pass from thee, and I will clothe thee with change of raiment."], [3, 5, "And I said, Let them set a fair mitre upon his head. So they set a fair mitre upon his head, and clothed him with garments. And the angel of the LORD stood by."], [3, 6, "And the angel of the LORD protested unto Joshua, saying,"], [3, 7, "Thus saith the LORD of hosts; If thou wilt walk in my ways, and if thou wilt keep my charge, then thou shalt also judge my house, and shalt also keep my courts, and I will give thee places to walk among these that stand by."]], "q": "After the new robes comes a covenant in verse 7: “If thou wilt walk in my ways.” How are being cleansed and keeping covenants connected?"}], "ck": "Zechariah saw a vision of a high priest named Joshua wearing dirty clothes. The Lord gave him clean, beautiful ones.", "cf": "Joshua was the real high priest of the returned exiles. His filthy garments stood for sin, and the new robes and clean turban for being cleansed and restored to serve. Covenants and temple clothing echo that same picture.", "wd": ["change of raiment", "מַחֲלָצוֹת", "machalatsot", "Hebrew", "fine festival robes, the kind put on for a celebration."]}, {"d": "2026-12-11", "w": 50, "t": "Not by might, but by my Spirit", "a": [{"r": "Zechariah 4:6", "v": [[4, 6, "Then he answered and spake unto me, saying, This is the word of the LORD unto Zerubbabel, saying, Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "God does big things with His Spirit, not with big muscles. How can the Holy Ghost help you?", "act": "Try to move a heavy chair alone, then together, and talk about how God helps us do hard things."}, {"r": "Zechariah 4:6–7, 10", "v": [[4, 6, "Then he answered and spake unto me, saying, This is the word of the LORD unto Zerubbabel, saying, Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts."], [4, 7, "Who art thou, O great mountain? before Zerubbabel thou shalt become a plain: and he shall bring forth the headstone thereof with shoutings, crying, Grace, grace unto it."], [4, 10, "For who hath despised the day of small things? for they shall rejoice, and shall see the plummet in the hand of Zerubbabel with those seven; they are the eyes of the LORD, which run to and fro through the whole earth."]], "q": "The temple seemed too hard to finish, like a mountain. What does God say will help?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 4:6–10", "v": [[4, 6, "Then he answered and spake unto me, saying, This is the word of the LORD unto Zerubbabel, saying, Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts."], [4, 7, "Who art thou, O great mountain? before Zerubbabel thou shalt become a plain: and he shall bring forth the headstone thereof with shoutings, crying, Grace, grace unto it."], [4, 8, "Moreover the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,"], [4, 9, "The hands of Zerubbabel have laid the foundation of this house; his hands shall also finish it; and thou shalt know that the LORD of hosts hath sent me unto you."], [4, 10, "For who hath despised the day of small things? for they shall rejoice, and shall see the plummet in the hand of Zerubbabel with those seven; they are the eyes of the LORD, which run to and fro through the whole earth."]], "q": "Verse 10 asks, “Who hath despised the day of small things?” Why do small beginnings matter to God?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 4:1–10", "v": [[4, 1, "And the angel that talked with me came again, and waked me, as a man that is wakened out of his sleep,"], [4, 2, "And said unto me, What seest thou? And I said, I have looked, and behold a candlestick all of gold, with a bowl upon the top of it, and his seven lamps thereon, and seven pipes to the seven lamps, which are upon the top thereof:"], [4, 3, "And two olive trees by it, one upon the right side of the bowl, and the other upon the left side thereof."], [4, 4, "So I answered and spake to the angel that talked with me, saying, What are these, my lord?"], [4, 5, "Then the angel that talked with me answered and said unto me, Knowest thou not what these be? And I said, No, my lord."], [4, 6, "Then he answered and spake unto me, saying, This is the word of the LORD unto Zerubbabel, saying, Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts."], [4, 7, "Who art thou, O great mountain? before Zerubbabel thou shalt become a plain: and he shall bring forth the headstone thereof with shoutings, crying, Grace, grace unto it."], [4, 8, "Moreover the word of the LORD came unto me, saying,"], [4, 9, "The hands of Zerubbabel have laid the foundation of this house; his hands shall also finish it; and thou shalt know that the LORD of hosts hath sent me unto you."], [4, 10, "For who hath despised the day of small things? for they shall rejoice, and shall see the plummet in the hand of Zerubbabel with those seven; they are the eyes of the LORD, which run to and fro through the whole earth."]], "q": "Zerubbabel faced a “great mountain” of opposition (verse 7). Where in your life do you need to hear “Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit”?"}], "ck": "Zerubbabel was the leader in charge of building the temple. It was a big job, and God told him how it would get done.", "cf": "Zerubbabel, a descendant of King David, led the rebuilding against discouragement and opposition. The temple was finished in 515 BC, about five years after Haggai and Zechariah began preaching.", "wd": ["might", "חַיִל", "chayil", "Hebrew", "strength, wealth or an army. Not by armies or money, but by the Spirit."]}, {"d": "2026-12-12", "w": 50, "t": "Your King comes", "a": [{"r": "Zechariah 9:9", "v": [[9, 9, "Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion; shout, O daughter of Jerusalem: behold, thy King cometh unto thee: he is just, and having salvation; lowly, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt the foal of an ass."]], "q": "Zechariah said a king would ride into Jerusalem on a donkey. Who rode a donkey into Jerusalem?", "act": "Act out Palm Sunday: wave paper or green leaves and say, “Hosanna!”"}, {"r": "Zechariah 9:9", "v": [[9, 9, "Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion; shout, O daughter of Jerusalem: behold, thy King cometh unto thee: he is just, and having salvation; lowly, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt the foal of an ass."]], "q": "Kings usually rode horses into battle. Why would the Savior choose to ride a donkey?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 9:9–10", "v": [[9, 9, "Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion; shout, O daughter of Jerusalem: behold, thy King cometh unto thee: he is just, and having salvation; lowly, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt the foal of an ass."], [9, 10, "And I will cut off the chariot from Ephraim, and the horse from Jerusalem, and the battle bow shall be cut off: and he shall speak peace unto the heathen: and his dominion shall be from sea even to sea, and from the river even to the ends of the earth."]], "q": "Matthew 21:4–5 quotes this verse when Jesus entered Jerusalem. What does verse 10 say about the kind of kingdom He brings?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 9:9–12", "v": [[9, 9, "Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion; shout, O daughter of Jerusalem: behold, thy King cometh unto thee: he is just, and having salvation; lowly, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt the foal of an ass."], [9, 10, "And I will cut off the chariot from Ephraim, and the horse from Jerusalem, and the battle bow shall be cut off: and he shall speak peace unto the heathen: and his dominion shall be from sea even to sea, and from the river even to the ends of the earth."], [9, 11, "As for thee also, by the blood of thy covenant I have sent forth thy prisoners out of the pit wherein is no water."], [9, 12, "Turn you to the strong hold, ye prisoners of hope: even to day do I declare that I will render double unto thee;"]], "q": "“Lowly, and riding upon an ass”: the Messiah comes humble, bringing salvation and peace. What does that teach about how He comes into our lives?"}], "ck": "About 500 years before Jesus was born, Zechariah told how the King would come to Jerusalem.", "cf": "In the ancient Near East, a king on a horse came for war; a donkey signaled peace. Jesus fulfilled this prophecy at His Triumphal Entry, the week before His death (Matthew 21:1–11).", "wd": ["lowly", "עָנִי", "ani", "Hebrew", "humble, poor, even afflicted. The King comes not proud and powerful but humble."]}, {"d": "2026-12-13", "w": 50, "t": "Holiness to the Lord", "a": [{"r": "Zechariah 14:20", "v": [[14, 20, "In that day shall there be upon the bells of the horses, HOLINESS UNTO THE LORD; and the pots in the LORD’s house shall be like the bowls before the altar."]], "q": "Zechariah saw a day when even horses' bells would say “Holiness unto the Lord.” Where can we see those words today?", "act": "Look at a picture of a temple and find the words “Holiness to the Lord.”"}, {"r": "Zechariah 14:9, 20–21", "v": [[14, 9, "And the LORD shall be king over all the earth: in that day shall there be one LORD, and his name one."], [14, 20, "In that day shall there be upon the bells of the horses, HOLINESS UNTO THE LORD; and the pots in the LORD’s house shall be like the bowls before the altar."], [14, 21, "Yea, every pot in Jerusalem and in Judah shall be holiness unto the LORD of hosts: and all they that sacrifice shall come and take of them, and seethe therein: and in that day there shall be no more the Canaanite in the house of the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "Even ordinary pots would be holy someday. What ordinary things in our home could we use for holy purposes?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 14:4, 9, 20–21", "v": [[14, 4, "And his feet shall stand in that day upon the mount of Olives, which is before Jerusalem on the east, and the mount of Olives shall cleave in the midst thereof toward the east and toward the west, and there shall be a very great valley; and half of the mountain shall remove toward the north, and half of it toward the south."], [14, 9, "And the LORD shall be king over all the earth: in that day shall there be one LORD, and his name one."], [14, 20, "In that day shall there be upon the bells of the horses, HOLINESS UNTO THE LORD; and the pots in the LORD’s house shall be like the bowls before the altar."], [14, 21, "Yea, every pot in Jerusalem and in Judah shall be holiness unto the LORD of hosts: and all they that sacrifice shall come and take of them, and seethe therein: and in that day there shall be no more the Canaanite in the house of the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "Verse 4 says the Lord's feet will stand on the mount of Olives. What does this chapter teach about His Second Coming?"}, {"r": "Zechariah 13:6; 14:4, 9, 20–21", "v": [[13, 6, "And one shall say unto him, What are these wounds in thine hands? Then he shall answer, Those with which I was wounded in the house of my friends."], [14, 4, "And his feet shall stand in that day upon the mount of Olives, which is before Jerusalem on the east, and the mount of Olives shall cleave in the midst thereof toward the east and toward the west, and there shall be a very great valley; and half of the mountain shall remove toward the north, and half of it toward the south."], [14, 9, "And the LORD shall be king over all the earth: in that day shall there be one LORD, and his name one."], [14, 20, "In that day shall there be upon the bells of the horses, HOLINESS UNTO THE LORD; and the pots in the LORD’s house shall be like the bowls before the altar."], [14, 21, "Yea, every pot in Jerusalem and in Judah shall be holiness unto the LORD of hosts: and all they that sacrifice shall come and take of them, and seethe therein: and in that day there shall be no more the Canaanite in the house of the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "Zechariah 13:6 speaks of “wounds in thine hands” (see D&C 45:51–52). How do these chapters point to Christ's return, and what would it mean for “holiness” to be written on everyday life?"}], "ck": "Zechariah saw what the world will be like when Jesus comes again. Everything will be holy.", "cf": "Latter-day Saint temples carry the words “Holiness to the Lord,” drawn from this verse and from the high priest's golden plate (Exodus 28:36). Zechariah pictures a day when that holiness spreads to bells and cooking pots.", "wd": ["holiness", "קֹדֶשׁ", "qodesh", "Hebrew", "set apart for God, belonging to Him."]}, {"d": "2026-12-14", "w": 51, "t": "I have loved you", "a": [{"r": "Malachi 1:2", "v": [[1, 2, "I have loved you, saith the LORD. Yet ye say, Wherein hast thou loved us? Was not Esau Jacob’s brother? saith the LORD: yet I loved Jacob,"]], "q": "The first thing God says in Malachi is “I have loved you.” How do you know Heavenly Father loves you?", "act": "Write “I have loved you” on a paper heart and tape it where everyone will see it."}, {"r": "Malachi 1:2", "v": [[1, 2, "I have loved you, saith the LORD. Yet ye say, Wherein hast thou loved us? Was not Esau Jacob’s brother? saith the LORD: yet I loved Jacob,"]], "q": "The people asked God, “Wherein hast thou loved us?” What would you answer if someone asked how God has shown love?"}, {"r": "Malachi 1:1–2", "v": [[1, 1, "The burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi."], [1, 2, "I have loved you, saith the LORD. Yet ye say, Wherein hast thou loved us? Was not Esau Jacob’s brother? saith the LORD: yet I loved Jacob,"]], "q": "Malachi is written as a back-and-forth: God speaks, the people push back with a question. Why might God start with love before any correction?"}, {"r": "Malachi 1:1–5", "v": [[1, 1, "The burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi."], [1, 2, "I have loved you, saith the LORD. Yet ye say, Wherein hast thou loved us? Was not Esau Jacob’s brother? saith the LORD: yet I loved Jacob,"], [1, 3, "And I hated Esau, and laid his mountains and his heritage waste for the dragons of the wilderness."], [1, 4, "Whereas Edom saith, We are impoverished, but we will return and build the desolate places; thus saith the LORD of hosts, They shall build, but I will throw down; and they shall call them, The border of wickedness, and, The people against whom the LORD hath indignation for ever."], [1, 5, "And your eyes shall see, and ye shall say, The LORD will be magnified from the border of Israel."]], "q": "Israel doubted God's love because life was hard. How do we see His love when circumstances don't seem to show it?"}], "ck": "Malachi is the very last book in the Old Testament. It starts with God telling His people He loves them.", "cf": "Malachi, whose name means “my messenger,” wrote around 450–430 BC, after the temple was rebuilt but people had grown careless. His book is built as a series of exchanges: God speaks, and the people ask, “Wherein?”", "wd": ["loved", "אָהַב", "ahav", "Hebrew", "to love, with affection and loyalty. The same word is used for loving God with all your heart (Deuteronomy 6:5)."]}, {"d": "2026-12-15", "w": 51, "t": "Our best for God", "a": [{"r": "Malachi 1:8", "v": [[1, 8, "And if ye offer the blind for sacrifice, is it not evil? and if ye offer the lame and sick, is it not evil? offer it now unto thy governor; will he be pleased with thee, or accept thy person? saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "People were giving God their sick and hurt animals instead of their best. How can we give God our best?", "act": "Pick out your favorite crayon or toy and use it to make something kind for someone else."}, {"r": "Malachi 1:6–8", "v": [[1, 6, "A son honoureth his father, and a servant his master: if then I be a father, where is mine honour? and if I be a master, where is my fear? saith the LORD of hosts unto you, O priests, that despise my name. And ye say, Wherein have we despised thy name?"], [1, 7, "Ye offer polluted bread upon mine altar; and ye say, Wherein have we polluted thee? In that ye say, The table of the LORD is contemptible."], [1, 8, "And if ye offer the blind for sacrifice, is it not evil? and if ye offer the lame and sick, is it not evil? offer it now unto thy governor; will he be pleased with thee, or accept thy person? saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "Malachi asks if people would give a sick animal as a gift to the governor. Why should our gifts to God be our best?"}, {"r": "Malachi 1:6–11", "v": [[1, 6, "A son honoureth his father, and a servant his master: if then I be a father, where is mine honour? and if I be a master, where is my fear? saith the LORD of hosts unto you, O priests, that despise my name. And ye say, Wherein have we despised thy name?"], [1, 7, "Ye offer polluted bread upon mine altar; and ye say, Wherein have we polluted thee? In that ye say, The table of the LORD is contemptible."], [1, 8, "And if ye offer the blind for sacrifice, is it not evil? and if ye offer the lame and sick, is it not evil? offer it now unto thy governor; will he be pleased with thee, or accept thy person? saith the LORD of hosts."], [1, 9, "And now, I pray you, beseech God that he will be gracious unto us: this hath been by your means: will he regard your persons? saith the LORD of hosts."], [1, 10, "Who is there even among you that would shut the doors for nought? neither do ye kindle fire on mine altar for nought. I have no pleasure in you, saith the LORD of hosts, neither will I accept an offering at your hand."], [1, 11, "For from the rising of the sun even unto the going down of the same my name shall be great among the Gentiles; and in every place incense shall be offered unto my name, and a pure offering: for my name shall be great among the heathen, saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "Verse 11 promises God's name will be great among all nations. What does a “pure offering” look like for us today?"}, {"r": "Malachi 1:6–14", "v": [[1, 6, "A son honoureth his father, and a servant his master: if then I be a father, where is mine honour? and if I be a master, where is my fear? saith the LORD of hosts unto you, O priests, that despise my name. And ye say, Wherein have we despised thy name?"], [1, 7, "Ye offer polluted bread upon mine altar; and ye say, Wherein have we polluted thee? In that ye say, The table of the LORD is contemptible."], [1, 8, "And if ye offer the blind for sacrifice, is it not evil? and if ye offer the lame and sick, is it not evil? offer it now unto thy governor; will he be pleased with thee, or accept thy person? saith the LORD of hosts."], [1, 9, "And now, I pray you, beseech God that he will be gracious unto us: this hath been by your means: will he regard your persons? saith the LORD of hosts."], [1, 10, "Who is there even among you that would shut the doors for nought? neither do ye kindle fire on mine altar for nought. I have no pleasure in you, saith the LORD of hosts, neither will I accept an offering at your hand."], [1, 11, "For from the rising of the sun even unto the going down of the same my name shall be great among the Gentiles; and in every place incense shall be offered unto my name, and a pure offering: for my name shall be great among the heathen, saith the LORD of hosts."], [1, 12, "But ye have profaned it, in that ye say, The table of the LORD is polluted; and the fruit thereof, even his meat, is contemptible."], [1, 13, "Ye said also, Behold, what a weariness is it! and ye have snuffed at it, saith the LORD of hosts; and ye brought that which was torn, and the lame, and the sick; thus ye brought an offering: should I accept this of your hand? saith the LORD."], [1, 14, "But cursed be the deceiver, which hath in his flock a male, and voweth, and sacrificeth unto the LORD a corrupt thing: for I am a great King, saith the LORD of hosts, and my name is dreadful among the heathen."]], "q": "The priests called worship “a weariness” (verse 13). When does worship start to feel like a chore, and what helps?"}], "ck": "Long ago people offered animals to God. They were supposed to give their best ones.", "cf": "The law required animals “without blemish” (Leviticus 22:20). Priests were accepting blind, lame and sick animals, the ones no one wanted, and calling it good enough.", "wd": ["offering", "מִנְחָה", "minchah", "Hebrew", "a gift or tribute, something you give to show honor."]}, {"d": "2026-12-16", "w": 51, "t": "The refiner's fire", "a": [{"r": "Malachi 3:2–3", "v": [[3, 2, "But who may abide the day of his coming? and who shall stand when he appeareth? for he is like a refiner’s fire, and like fullers’ soap:"], [3, 3, "And he shall sit as a refiner and purifier of silver: and he shall purify the sons of Levi, and purge them as gold and silver, that they may offer unto the LORD an offering in righteousness."]], "q": "A refiner heats silver until it shines. Heavenly Father helps us become better, even when it's hard. What helps you grow?", "act": "Polish a spoon or a coin until it shines and talk about how God helps us shine."}, {"r": "Malachi 3:1–3", "v": [[3, 1, "Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me: and the Lord, whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple, even the messenger of the covenant, whom ye delight in: behold, he shall come, saith the LORD of hosts."], [3, 2, "But who may abide the day of his coming? and who shall stand when he appeareth? for he is like a refiner’s fire, and like fullers’ soap:"], [3, 3, "And he shall sit as a refiner and purifier of silver: and he shall purify the sons of Levi, and purge them as gold and silver, that they may offer unto the LORD an offering in righteousness."]], "q": "Malachi compares the Savior to a refiner's fire and to strong soap. What do fire and soap do?"}, {"r": "Malachi 3:1–3", "v": [[3, 1, "Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me: and the Lord, whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple, even the messenger of the covenant, whom ye delight in: behold, he shall come, saith the LORD of hosts."], [3, 2, "But who may abide the day of his coming? and who shall stand when he appeareth? for he is like a refiner’s fire, and like fullers’ soap:"], [3, 3, "And he shall sit as a refiner and purifier of silver: and he shall purify the sons of Levi, and purge them as gold and silver, that they may offer unto the LORD an offering in righteousness."]], "q": "Verse 3 says He “shall sit as a refiner.” Why might it matter that the refiner sits and watches?"}, {"r": "Malachi 3:1–3", "v": [[3, 1, "Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me: and the Lord, whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple, even the messenger of the covenant, whom ye delight in: behold, he shall come, saith the LORD of hosts."], [3, 2, "But who may abide the day of his coming? and who shall stand when he appeareth? for he is like a refiner’s fire, and like fullers’ soap:"], [3, 3, "And he shall sit as a refiner and purifier of silver: and he shall purify the sons of Levi, and purge them as gold and silver, that they may offer unto the LORD an offering in righteousness."]], "q": "Jesus quoted verse 1 about John the Baptist (Matthew 11:10), and Malachi 3 was given to the Nephites (3 Nephi 24). What dross do you hope He refines out of you?"}], "ck": "Malachi said the Savior would come to His temple to make people clean and pure.", "cf": "Silver was refined by heating it until impurities, called dross, rose to the top and could be skimmed off. A refiner watched closely so the silver wasn't harmed. Fullers washed and whitened cloth with strong soap.", "wd": ["refiner", "צָרַף", "tsaraf", "Hebrew", "to melt and purify metal, testing it until only the pure part is left."]}, {"d": "2026-12-17", "w": 51, "t": "Windows of heaven", "a": [{"r": "Malachi 3:10", "v": [[3, 10, "Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house, and prove me now herewith, saith the LORD of hosts, if I will not open you the windows of heaven, and pour you out a blessing, that there shall not be room enough to receive it."]], "q": "God promises to open the windows of heaven for those who pay tithing. What blessings has God given our family?", "act": "Count out ten coins or candies, and set one aside as the Lord's tenth."}, {"r": "Malachi 3:8–10", "v": [[3, 8, "Will a man rob God? Yet ye have robbed me. But ye say, Wherein have we robbed thee? In tithes and offerings."], [3, 9, "Ye are cursed with a curse: for ye have robbed me, even this whole nation."], [3, 10, "Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house, and prove me now herewith, saith the LORD of hosts, if I will not open you the windows of heaven, and pour you out a blessing, that there shall not be room enough to receive it."]], "q": "Tithing is giving one tenth to the Lord. Why do you think God asks us to give back?"}, {"r": "Malachi 3:8–12", "v": [[3, 8, "Will a man rob God? Yet ye have robbed me. But ye say, Wherein have we robbed thee? In tithes and offerings."], [3, 9, "Ye are cursed with a curse: for ye have robbed me, even this whole nation."], [3, 10, "Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house, and prove me now herewith, saith the LORD of hosts, if I will not open you the windows of heaven, and pour you out a blessing, that there shall not be room enough to receive it."], [3, 11, "And I will rebuke the devourer for your sakes, and he shall not destroy the fruits of your ground; neither shall your vine cast her fruit before the time in the field, saith the LORD of hosts."], [3, 12, "And all nations shall call you blessed: for ye shall be a delightsome land, saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "God says, “Prove me now herewith.” He invites us to test Him. Why would God invite that?"}, {"r": "Malachi 3:7–12", "v": [[3, 7, "Even from the days of your fathers ye are gone away from mine ordinances, and have not kept them. Return unto me, and I will return unto you, saith the LORD of hosts. But ye said, Wherein shall we return?"], [3, 8, "Will a man rob God? Yet ye have robbed me. But ye say, Wherein have we robbed thee? In tithes and offerings."], [3, 9, "Ye are cursed with a curse: for ye have robbed me, even this whole nation."], [3, 10, "Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house, and prove me now herewith, saith the LORD of hosts, if I will not open you the windows of heaven, and pour you out a blessing, that there shall not be room enough to receive it."], [3, 11, "And I will rebuke the devourer for your sakes, and he shall not destroy the fruits of your ground; neither shall your vine cast her fruit before the time in the field, saith the LORD of hosts."], [3, 12, "And all nations shall call you blessed: for ye shall be a delightsome land, saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "Before tithing comes “Return unto me, and I will return unto you” (verse 7). How is tithing about returning to God, not just money?"}], "ck": "Malachi taught about tithing: giving a tenth of what we earn to God.", "cf": "In ancient Israel the tithe was a tenth of crops and flocks, brought to the storehouse to support the Levites and the poor. The Savior gave these same words to the Nephites (3 Nephi 24:10).", "wd": ["tithe", "מַעֲשֵׂר", "ma'aser", "Hebrew", "a tenth part."]}, {"d": "2026-12-18", "w": 51, "t": "God's book of remembrance", "a": [{"r": "Malachi 3:16–17", "v": [[3, 16, "Then they that feared the LORD spake often one to another: and the LORD hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared the LORD, and that thought upon his name."], [3, 17, "And they shall be mine, saith the LORD of hosts, in that day when I make up my jewels; and I will spare them, as a man spareth his own son that serveth him."]], "q": "God keeps a book that remembers people who love Him. He calls them His jewels. Do you know you're one of His jewels?", "act": "Make a family “book of remembrance” page: draw something good that happened this week."}, {"r": "Malachi 3:16–17", "v": [[3, 16, "Then they that feared the LORD spake often one to another: and the LORD hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared the LORD, and that thought upon his name."], [3, 17, "And they shall be mine, saith the LORD of hosts, in that day when I make up my jewels; and I will spare them, as a man spareth his own son that serveth him."]], "q": "The faithful “spake often one to another.” Why does talking about God together matter?"}, {"r": "Malachi 3:13–18", "v": [[3, 13, "Your words have been stout against me, saith the LORD. Yet ye say, What have we spoken so much against thee?"], [3, 14, "Ye have said, It is vain to serve God: and what profit is it that we have kept his ordinance, and that we have walked mournfully before the LORD of hosts?"], [3, 15, "And now we call the proud happy; yea, they that work wickedness are set up; yea, they that tempt God are even delivered."], [3, 16, "Then they that feared the LORD spake often one to another: and the LORD hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared the LORD, and that thought upon his name."], [3, 17, "And they shall be mine, saith the LORD of hosts, in that day when I make up my jewels; and I will spare them, as a man spareth his own son that serveth him."], [3, 18, "Then shall ye return, and discern between the righteous and the wicked, between him that serveth God and him that serveth him not."]], "q": "Some people said serving God was useless (verse 14). What did God do for those who stayed faithful?"}, {"r": "Malachi 3:13–18", "v": [[3, 13, "Your words have been stout against me, saith the LORD. Yet ye say, What have we spoken so much against thee?"], [3, 14, "Ye have said, It is vain to serve God: and what profit is it that we have kept his ordinance, and that we have walked mournfully before the LORD of hosts?"], [3, 15, "And now we call the proud happy; yea, they that work wickedness are set up; yea, they that tempt God are even delivered."], [3, 16, "Then they that feared the LORD spake often one to another: and the LORD hearkened, and heard it, and a book of remembrance was written before him for them that feared the LORD, and that thought upon his name."], [3, 17, "And they shall be mine, saith the LORD of hosts, in that day when I make up my jewels; and I will spare them, as a man spareth his own son that serveth him."], [3, 18, "Then shall ye return, and discern between the righteous and the wicked, between him that serveth God and him that serveth him not."]], "q": "Latter-day Saints have long kept books of remembrance. What would you want remembered about your faith, and who in your family should hear it?"}], "ck": "God remembers everyone who loves Him, and He treasures them.", "cf": "When some complained that the proud seemed to win, the faithful encouraged one another, and the Lord wrote their names in “a book of remembrance.” He calls them His “jewels,” His treasured possession.", "wd": ["jewels", "סְגֻלָּה", "segullah", "Hebrew", "a treasured possession, what a king keeps for himself. Israel is called this in Exodus 19:5, “a peculiar treasure.”"]}, {"d": "2026-12-19", "w": 51, "t": "Healing in His wings", "a": [{"r": "Malachi 4:2", "v": [[4, 2, "But unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings; and ye shall go forth, and grow up as calves of the stall."]], "q": "Malachi calls Jesus the “Sun of Righteousness.” Just like the sun warms us, Jesus helps us feel better. How does Jesus help you?", "act": "Sit together in a sunny window or under a lamp and feel the warmth while you talk about Jesus."}, {"r": "Malachi 4:2", "v": [[4, 2, "But unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings; and ye shall go forth, and grow up as calves of the stall."]], "q": "What does it mean that Jesus has “healing in his wings”?"}, {"r": "Malachi 4:1–2", "v": [[4, 1, "For, behold, the day cometh, that shall burn as an oven; and all the proud, yea, and all that do wickedly, shall be stubble: and the day that cometh shall burn them up, saith the LORD of hosts, that it shall leave them neither root nor branch."], [4, 2, "But unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings; and ye shall go forth, and grow up as calves of the stall."]], "q": "Verse 2 says those healed will “grow up as calves of the stall,” jumping and free. What does healing from Jesus feel like?"}, {"r": "Malachi 4:1–3", "v": [[4, 1, "For, behold, the day cometh, that shall burn as an oven; and all the proud, yea, and all that do wickedly, shall be stubble: and the day that cometh shall burn them up, saith the LORD of hosts, that it shall leave them neither root nor branch."], [4, 2, "But unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings; and ye shall go forth, and grow up as calves of the stall."], [4, 3, "And ye shall tread down the wicked; for they shall be ashes under the soles of your feet in the day that I shall do this, saith the LORD of hosts."]], "q": "The same day burns the proud like stubble and heals those who fear His name. What makes the difference between the two?"}], "ck": "Malachi said that when Jesus comes, He will bring healing like the warm sun rising in the morning.", "cf": "The rising sun brings warmth and light. In Hebrew, “wings” can also mean the corners of a robe, where the tassels were. The woman who touched the “hem” of Jesus' garment was healed (Matthew 9:20–22).", "wd": ["wings", "כְּנָפַיִם", "kenafayim", "Hebrew", "wings, or the corners of a garment. The same word is used for the corners of the robe where tassels hung."]}, {"d": "2026-12-20", "w": 51, "t": "Hearts turned to families", "a": [{"r": "Malachi 4:5–6", "v": [[4, 5, "Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD:"], [4, 6, "And he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse."]], "q": "Elijah would come to help families love each other forever. Who in our family would you like to learn about?", "act": "Look at old family photos and tell a story about a grandparent or ancestor."}, {"r": "Malachi 4:5–6", "v": [[4, 5, "Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD:"], [4, 6, "And he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse."]], "q": "Elijah came to Joseph Smith and Oliver Cowdery in the Kirtland Temple in 1836 (D&C 110:13–16). What did he bring back?"}, {"r": "Malachi 4:5–6", "v": [[4, 5, "Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD:"], [4, 6, "And he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse."]], "q": "Moroni quoted these verses to Joseph Smith a little differently: Elijah would reveal the priesthood (D&C 2). Why do families need that priesthood power?"}, {"r": "Malachi 4:4–6", "v": [[4, 4, "Remember ye the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded unto him in Horeb for all Israel, with the statutes and judgments."], [4, 5, "Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD:"], [4, 6, "And he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse."]], "q": "The Old Testament ends with this promise. How does it lead straight into the Restoration and the work we do in temples today?"}], "ck": "Elijah was a great prophet. Malachi promised he would come back before the Second Coming.", "cf": "These are the last words of the Old Testament. Moroni quoted them to Joseph Smith in 1823 (Joseph Smith—History 1:38–39), and Elijah appeared in the Kirtland Temple on April 3, 1836, during Passover week, when Jewish families set a place for Elijah.", "wd": ["Elijah", "אֵלִיָּהוּ", "Eliyahu", "Hebrew", "“My God is Jehovah.” His name itself is a testimony."]}, {"d": "2026-12-21", "w": 52, "t": "God with us", "a": [{"r": "Isaiah 7:14", "v": [[7, 14, "Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel."]], "q": "Isaiah promised a baby called Immanuel. Immanuel means “God with us.” How does Jesus show that God is with us?", "act": "Make a paper chain and on each link write a way Jesus is with us."}, {"r": "Isaiah 7:14", "v": [[7, 14, "Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel."]], "q": "Matthew says this verse was fulfilled when Jesus was born to Mary (Matthew 1:22–23). What does the name Immanuel teach about Jesus?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 7:14", "v": [[7, 14, "Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel."]], "q": "Isaiah gave this sign to a frightened king about 700 years before Jesus. Why might God give a sign of a baby instead of an army?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 7:10–14", "v": [[7, 10, "Moreover the LORD spake again unto Ahaz, saying,"], [7, 11, "Ask thee a sign of the LORD thy God; ask it either in the depth, or in the height above."], [7, 12, "But Ahaz said, I will not ask, neither will I tempt the LORD."], [7, 13, "And he said, Hear ye now, O house of David; Is it a small thing for you to weary men, but will ye weary my God also?"], [7, 14, "Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel."]], "q": "King Ahaz refused to ask for a sign, and God gave one anyway (verses 11–14). What does “God with us” mean to you this Christmas?"}], "ck": "Isaiah was a prophet who lived a long time before Jesus. He told about Jesus' birth.", "cf": "Around 735 BC, King Ahaz of Judah feared an attack from Israel and Syria. Isaiah offered him a sign from God, and when Ahaz refused, Isaiah gave this one. Matthew 1:23 applies it to the birth of Jesus.", "wd": ["Immanuel", "עִמָּנוּ אֵל", "Immanu El", "Hebrew", "“With us is God.”"]}, {"d": "2026-12-22", "w": 52, "t": "His name shall be called", "a": [{"r": "Isaiah 9:6", "v": [[9, 6, "For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counseller, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace."]], "q": "Isaiah gave Jesus beautiful names: Wonderful, Counsellor, Prince of Peace. Which name do you like best?", "act": "Write each name of Jesus on a star and hang them up."}, {"r": "Isaiah 9:2, 6", "v": [[9, 2, "The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light: they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined."], [9, 6, "For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counseller, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace."]], "q": "Verse 2 talks about a great light. How is Jesus like a light in the dark?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 9:2, 6–7", "v": [[9, 2, "The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light: they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined."], [9, 6, "For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counseller, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace."], [9, 7, "Of the increase of his government and peace there shall be no end, upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it, and to establish it with judgment and with justice from henceforth even for ever. The zeal of the LORD of hosts will perform this."]], "q": "Each name in verse 6 tells something about Him. What does each one teach you?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 9:1–7", "v": [[9, 1, "Nevertheless the dimness shall not be such as was in her vexation, when at the first he lightly afflicted the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali, and afterward did more grievously afflict her by the way of the sea, beyond Jordan, in Galilee of the nations."], [9, 2, "The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light: they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined."], [9, 3, "Thou hast multiplied the nation, and not increased the joy: they joy before thee according to the joy in harvest, and as men rejoice when they divide the spoil."], [9, 4, "For thou hast broken the yoke of his burden, and the staff of his shoulder, the rod of his oppressor, as in the day of Midian."], [9, 5, "For every battle of the warrior is with confused noise, and garments rolled in blood; but this shall be with burning and fuel of fire."], [9, 6, "For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counseller, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace."], [9, 7, "Of the increase of his government and peace there shall be no end, upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom, to order it, and to establish it with judgment and with justice from henceforth even for ever. The zeal of the LORD of hosts will perform this."]], "q": "Matthew 4:13–16 applies verses 1–2 to Jesus' ministry in Galilee. Which of His names have you come to know by experience?"}], "ck": "Isaiah used special names for Jesus to help us know who He is.", "cf": "Galilee, in the north, was the first part of Israel conquered by Assyria and was called “Galilee of the nations.” Isaiah promised a great light there, and Jesus began His ministry in Galilee.", "wd": ["Wonderful", "פֶּלֶא", "pele", "Hebrew", "a wonder, a marvel, something only God can do."]}, {"d": "2026-12-23", "w": 52, "t": "A rod out of Jesse", "a": [{"r": "Isaiah 11:1", "v": [[11, 1, "And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:"]], "q": "Isaiah said a new branch would grow from an old tree stump. Jesus came from King David's family, like a new branch. What new things grow in winter?", "act": "Draw a tree stump with a small green branch growing out of it."}, {"r": "Isaiah 11:1–5", "v": [[11, 1, "And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:"], [11, 2, "And the spirit of the LORD shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and might, the spirit of knowledge and of the fear of the LORD;"], [11, 3, "And shall make him of quick understanding in the fear of the LORD: and he shall not judge after the sight of his eyes, neither reprove after the hearing of his ears:"], [11, 4, "But with righteousness shall he judge the poor, and reprove with equity for the meek of the earth: and he shall smite the earth with the rod of his mouth, and with the breath of his lips shall he slay the wicked."], [11, 5, "And righteousness shall be the girdle of his loins, and faithfulness the girdle of his reins."]], "q": "Jesse was King David's father. Why would Isaiah describe the Savior as a branch from Jesse's family?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 11:1–6", "v": [[11, 1, "And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:"], [11, 2, "And the spirit of the LORD shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and might, the spirit of knowledge and of the fear of the LORD;"], [11, 3, "And shall make him of quick understanding in the fear of the LORD: and he shall not judge after the sight of his eyes, neither reprove after the hearing of his ears:"], [11, 4, "But with righteousness shall he judge the poor, and reprove with equity for the meek of the earth: and he shall smite the earth with the rod of his mouth, and with the breath of his lips shall he slay the wicked."], [11, 5, "And righteousness shall be the girdle of his loins, and faithfulness the girdle of his reins."], [11, 6, "The wolf also shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; and the calf and the young lion and the fatling together; and a little child shall lead them."]], "q": "Verses 6–9 picture wolves and lambs living in peace. What does this teach about Christ's kingdom?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 11:1–10", "v": [[11, 1, "And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:"], [11, 2, "And the spirit of the LORD shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and might, the spirit of knowledge and of the fear of the LORD;"], [11, 3, "And shall make him of quick understanding in the fear of the LORD: and he shall not judge after the sight of his eyes, neither reprove after the hearing of his ears:"], [11, 4, "But with righteousness shall he judge the poor, and reprove with equity for the meek of the earth: and he shall smite the earth with the rod of his mouth, and with the breath of his lips shall he slay the wicked."], [11, 5, "And righteousness shall be the girdle of his loins, and faithfulness the girdle of his reins."], [11, 6, "The wolf also shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid; and the calf and the young lion and the fatling together; and a little child shall lead them."], [11, 7, "And the cow and the bear shall feed; their young ones shall lie down together: and the lion shall eat straw like the ox."], [11, 8, "And the sucking child shall play on the hole of the asp, and the weaned child shall put his hand on the cockatrice’ den."], [11, 9, "They shall not hurt nor destroy in all my holy mountain: for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea."], [11, 10, "And in that day there shall be a root of Jesse, which shall stand for an ensign of the people; to it shall the Gentiles seek: and his rest shall be glorious."]], "q": "Moroni quoted this chapter to Joseph Smith and said it was about to be fulfilled (Joseph Smith—History 1:40). How does it describe both Christ and the latter days (see D&C 113:1–6)?"}], "ck": "Isaiah talked about a tree that was cut down, and then a new little branch grew out of the stump.", "cf": "By Jesus' time, David's royal family had lost its throne, like a tree cut down to a stump. The branch growing from Jesse's roots is the Messiah, a descendant of David. Matthew and Luke both trace Jesus' line to David.", "wd": ["Branch", "נֵצֶר", "netser", "Hebrew", "a shoot or sprout. Some see a connection with Nazareth, Jesus' hometown, and Matthew 2:23."]}, {"d": "2026-12-24", "w": 52, "t": "Good tidings of great joy", "a": [{"r": "Luke 2:8–11", "v": [[2, 8, "And there were in the same country shepherds abiding in the field, keeping watch over their flock by night."], [2, 9, "And, lo, the angel of the Lord came upon them, and the glory of the Lord shone round about them: and they were sore afraid."], [2, 10, "And the angel said unto them, Fear not: for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people."], [2, 11, "For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord."]], "q": "Angels told shepherds that Jesus was born. If an angel came to tell you good news, how would you feel?", "act": "Act out the shepherds: one person is the angel, and everyone else hurries to see the baby."}, {"r": "Luke 2:8–14", "v": [[2, 8, "And there were in the same country shepherds abiding in the field, keeping watch over their flock by night."], [2, 9, "And, lo, the angel of the Lord came upon them, and the glory of the Lord shone round about them: and they were sore afraid."], [2, 10, "And the angel said unto them, Fear not: for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people."], [2, 11, "For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord."], [2, 12, "And this shall be a sign unto you; Ye shall find the babe wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger."], [2, 13, "And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host praising God, and saying,"], [2, 14, "Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men."]], "q": "The angels came to shepherds first. Why do you think God told shepherds?"}, {"r": "Luke 2:8–16", "v": [[2, 8, "And there were in the same country shepherds abiding in the field, keeping watch over their flock by night."], [2, 9, "And, lo, the angel of the Lord came upon them, and the glory of the Lord shone round about them: and they were sore afraid."], [2, 10, "And the angel said unto them, Fear not: for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people."], [2, 11, "For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord."], [2, 12, "And this shall be a sign unto you; Ye shall find the babe wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger."], [2, 13, "And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host praising God, and saying,"], [2, 14, "Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men."], [2, 15, "And it came to pass, as the angels were gone away from them into heaven, the shepherds said one to another, Let us now go even unto Bethlehem, and see this thing which is come to pass, which the Lord hath made known unto us."], [2, 16, "And they came with haste, and found Mary, and Joseph, and the babe lying in a manger."]], "q": "The shepherds went “with haste” (verse 16). What do we do when we really believe good news?"}, {"r": "Luke 2:8–20", "v": [[2, 8, "And there were in the same country shepherds abiding in the field, keeping watch over their flock by night."], [2, 9, "And, lo, the angel of the Lord came upon them, and the glory of the Lord shone round about them: and they were sore afraid."], [2, 10, "And the angel said unto them, Fear not: for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people."], [2, 11, "For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord."], [2, 12, "And this shall be a sign unto you; Ye shall find the babe wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger."], [2, 13, "And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host praising God, and saying,"], [2, 14, "Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men."], [2, 15, "And it came to pass, as the angels were gone away from them into heaven, the shepherds said one to another, Let us now go even unto Bethlehem, and see this thing which is come to pass, which the Lord hath made known unto us."], [2, 16, "And they came with haste, and found Mary, and Joseph, and the babe lying in a manger."], [2, 17, "And when they had seen it, they made known abroad the saying which was told them concerning this child."], [2, 18, "And all they that heard it wondered at those things which were told them by the shepherds."], [2, 19, "But Mary kept all these things, and pondered them in her heart."], [2, 20, "And the shepherds returned, glorifying and praising God for all the things that they had heard and seen, as it was told unto them."]], "q": "Verse 20 says the shepherds returned “glorifying and praising God for all the things that they had heard and seen.” How can we come away from Christmas like that?"}], "ck": "On the night Jesus was born, angels appeared to shepherds who were watching their sheep.", "cf": "Shepherds were poor and often looked down on. Flocks near Bethlehem may have included lambs raised for temple sacrifice. The first to hear of the Lamb of God were those who cared for lambs.", "wd": ["good tidings", "εὐαγγελίζομαι", "euangelizomai", "Greek", "to announce good news. It's where the words “evangelist” and “gospel” (good news) come from."]}, {"d": "2026-12-25", "w": 52, "t": "The baby in the manger", "a": [{"r": "Luke 2:7", "v": [[2, 7, "And she brought forth her firstborn son, and wrapped him in swaddling clothes, and laid him in a manger; because there was no room for them in the inn."]], "q": "Jesus was born and laid in a manger. A manger is where animals eat. Why was there no room at the inn?", "act": "Set up a nativity, or make one from cardboard, and place baby Jesus in the manger together."}, {"r": "Luke 2:1–7", "v": [[2, 1, "And it came to pass in those days, that there went out a decree from Cæsar Augustus, that all the world should be taxed."], [2, 2, "(And this taxing was first made when Cyrenius was governor of Syria.)"], [2, 3, "And all went to be taxed, every one into his own city."], [2, 4, "And Joseph also went up from Galilee, out of the city of Nazareth, into Judæa, unto the city of David, which is called Bethlehem; (because he was of the house and lineage of David:)"], [2, 5, "To be taxed with Mary his espoused wife, being great with child."], [2, 6, "And so it was, that, while they were there, the days were accomplished that she should be delivered."], [2, 7, "And she brought forth her firstborn son, and wrapped him in swaddling clothes, and laid him in a manger; because there was no room for them in the inn."]], "q": "Mary and Joseph traveled a long way to Bethlehem. What does it teach us that the King of kings was born in such a humble place?"}, {"r": "Luke 2:1–7", "v": [[2, 1, "And it came to pass in those days, that there went out a decree from Cæsar Augustus, that all the world should be taxed."], [2, 2, "(And this taxing was first made when Cyrenius was governor of Syria.)"], [2, 3, "And all went to be taxed, every one into his own city."], [2, 4, "And Joseph also went up from Galilee, out of the city of Nazareth, into Judæa, unto the city of David, which is called Bethlehem; (because he was of the house and lineage of David:)"], [2, 5, "To be taxed with Mary his espoused wife, being great with child."], [2, 6, "And so it was, that, while they were there, the days were accomplished that she should be delivered."], [2, 7, "And she brought forth her firstborn son, and wrapped him in swaddling clothes, and laid him in a manger; because there was no room for them in the inn."]], "q": "Luke tells exactly when this happened, under Caesar Augustus (verse 1). Why might he want to show it was a real event in history?"}, {"r": "Luke 2:1–7", "v": [[2, 1, "And it came to pass in those days, that there went out a decree from Cæsar Augustus, that all the world should be taxed."], [2, 2, "(And this taxing was first made when Cyrenius was governor of Syria.)"], [2, 3, "And all went to be taxed, every one into his own city."], [2, 4, "And Joseph also went up from Galilee, out of the city of Nazareth, into Judæa, unto the city of David, which is called Bethlehem; (because he was of the house and lineage of David:)"], [2, 5, "To be taxed with Mary his espoused wife, being great with child."], [2, 6, "And so it was, that, while they were there, the days were accomplished that she should be delivered."], [2, 7, "And she brought forth her firstborn son, and wrapped him in swaddling clothes, and laid him in a manger; because there was no room for them in the inn."]], "q": "He who made the world had no room in it (John 1:10–11). How do we make room for Him in our lives and our home?"}], "ck": "Today we celebrate the birth of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.", "cf": "Caesar Augustus ruled Rome from 27 BC to AD 14. The “inn” may have been a guest room in a crowded home, and animals were often kept in the lower part of the house, which is where a manger would be.", "wd": ["manger", "φάτνη", "phatnē", "Greek", "a feeding trough for animals."]}, {"d": "2026-12-26", "w": 52, "t": "He carried our sorrows", "a": [{"r": "Isaiah 53:4", "v": [[53, 4, "Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted."]], "q": "Jesus feels our sad and hurt feelings, and He helps carry them. Who can you tell when you feel sad?", "act": "Have one person carry a heavy bag, then let others help carry it, and talk about how Jesus helps carry our burdens."}, {"r": "Isaiah 53:3–5", "v": [[53, 3, "He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not."], [53, 4, "Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted."], [53, 5, "But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed."]], "q": "Isaiah wrote about Jesus' suffering long before it happened. What does “with his stripes we are healed” mean?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 53:3–6", "v": [[53, 3, "He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not."], [53, 4, "Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted."], [53, 5, "But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed."], [53, 6, "All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all."]], "q": "Verse 6 says “all we like sheep have gone astray.” What does the Savior do about that?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 53:1–12", "v": [[53, 1, "Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed?"], [53, 2, "For he shall grow up before him as a tender plant, and as a root out of a dry ground: he hath no form nor comeliness; and when we shall see him, there is no beauty that we should desire him."], [53, 3, "He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not."], [53, 4, "Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted."], [53, 5, "But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed."], [53, 6, "All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all."], [53, 7, "He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth."], [53, 8, "He was taken from prison and from judgment: and who shall declare his generation? for he was cut off out of the land of the living: for the transgression of my people was he stricken."], [53, 9, "And he made his grave with the wicked, and with the rich in his death; because he had done no violence, neither was any deceit in his mouth."], [53, 10, "Yet it pleased the LORD to bruise him; he hath put him to grief: when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days, and the pleasure of the LORD shall prosper in his hand."], [53, 11, "He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be satisfied: by his knowledge shall my righteous servant justify many; for he shall bear their iniquities."], [53, 12, "Therefore will I divide him a portion with the great, and he shall divide the spoil with the strong; because he hath poured out his soul unto death: and he was numbered with the transgressors; and he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors."]], "q": "Abinadi quoted this whole chapter to King Noah's priests (Mosiah 14). Why does Christmas lead naturally to Gethsemane and the cross?"}], "ck": "Isaiah wrote about how Jesus would suffer for us so that we could be healed.", "cf": "Isaiah 53 describes the “suffering servant.” The New Testament applies it to Jesus many times (for example Acts 8:32–35), and Abinadi quoted it in full in the Book of Mormon.", "wd": ["griefs", "חֳלִי", "choli", "Hebrew", "sicknesses. He bore not only our sins but our illnesses and pains (see Alma 7:11–12)."]}, {"d": "2026-12-27", "w": 52, "t": "We have waited for Him", "a": [{"r": "Isaiah 25:9", "v": [[25, 9, "And it shall be said in that day, Lo, this is our God; we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the LORD; we have waited for him, we will be glad and rejoice in his salvation."]], "q": "Isaiah said people will say, “This is our God; we have waited for him.” What are you excited for Jesus to do when He comes again?", "act": "Make a countdown chain for something good coming up, and talk about waiting with hope."}, {"r": "Isaiah 25:8–9", "v": [[25, 8, "He will swallow up death in victory; and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from off all faces; and the rebuke of his people shall he take away from off all the earth: for the LORD hath spoken it."], [25, 9, "And it shall be said in that day, Lo, this is our God; we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the LORD; we have waited for him, we will be glad and rejoice in his salvation."]], "q": "God will wipe away tears from all faces. What would it be like to have no more sadness?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 25:6–9", "v": [[25, 6, "And in this mountain shall the LORD of hosts make unto all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined."], [25, 7, "And he will destroy in this mountain the face of the covering cast over all people, and the vail that is spread over all nations."], [25, 8, "He will swallow up death in victory; and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from off all faces; and the rebuke of his people shall he take away from off all the earth: for the LORD hath spoken it."], [25, 9, "And it shall be said in that day, Lo, this is our God; we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the LORD; we have waited for him, we will be glad and rejoice in his salvation."]], "q": "Verse 8 says He will “swallow up death in victory.” How does Jesus' Resurrection fulfill that?"}, {"r": "Isaiah 25:1–9", "v": [[25, 1, "O LORD, thou art my God; I will exalt thee, I will praise thy name; for thou hast done wonderful things; thy counsels of old are faithfulness and truth."], [25, 2, "For thou hast made of a city an heap; of a defenced city a ruin: a palace of strangers to be no city; it shall never be built."], [25, 3, "Therefore shall the strong people glorify thee, the city of the terrible nations shall fear thee."], [25, 4, "For thou hast been a strength to the poor, a strength to the needy in his distress, a refuge from the storm, a shadow from the heat, when the blast of the terrible ones is as a storm against the wall."], [25, 5, "Thou shalt bring down the noise of strangers, as the heat in a dry place; even the heat with the shadow of a cloud: the branch of the terrible ones shall be brought low."], [25, 6, "And in this mountain shall the LORD of hosts make unto all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined."], [25, 7, "And he will destroy in this mountain the face of the covering cast over all people, and the vail that is spread over all nations."], [25, 8, "He will swallow up death in victory; and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from off all faces; and the rebuke of his people shall he take away from off all the earth: for the LORD hath spoken it."], [25, 9, "And it shall be said in that day, Lo, this is our God; we have waited for him, and he will save us: this is the LORD; we have waited for him, we will be glad and rejoice in his salvation."]], "q": "The Old Testament is full of people waiting for the Messiah. What are you waiting on the Lord for now, and what does this verse promise?"}], "ck": "Israel waited a long time for Jesus to come, and now we wait for Him to come again.", "cf": "Paul quotes verse 8 in his great chapter on the Resurrection (1 Corinthians 15:54). The Old Testament ends looking forward, and this verse gives this week its title.", "wd": ["waited", "קִוִּינוּ", "qivvinu", "Hebrew", "we waited with hope. The root word is related to a cord or rope, like hope that holds on."]}];
